WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:13.480
SHARP: Welcome.

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:14.820
I'm Philip Sharp.

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:19.170
And it is a real pleasure to be
able to introduce my colleague

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:22.660
Eric Lander to present a
talk on the historic event

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:25.890
of the sequencing
of the human genome.

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:28.560
When historians look
back on these decades

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:30.810
of biological
science that bracket

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:34.290
the change of the century
and the millennium,

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:37.020
the most noted advance
will be the sequencing

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:38.810
of the human genome.

00:00:38.810 --> 00:00:43.020
This sequence will shape all
biological and medical science

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:45.360
that is done postgenomic.

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:47.750
And, in fact, it is the
definition of that word,

00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:49.770
"postgenomic."

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:52.440
The consequences of
having the sequence

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:56.960
will not be fully realized
until decades have passed.

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:00.140
There are many stories
for MIT in this event.

00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:04.310
First there's the story of Eric
Lander, educated at Princeton

00:01:04.310 --> 00:01:09.200
as an undergraduate, PhD
in Mathematics at Oxford,

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:10.840
then a faculty
member at Harvard,

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:12.740
in the Business School.

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:15.170
His first relationship
was with MIT

00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:17.060
as a fellow in the
Whitehead Institute,

00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:19.010
studying human genetics.

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:21.200
Shortly thereafter,
he became a professor

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:24.560
in the Department of Biology
and a member of the Whitehead

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:26.180
Institute.

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:29.570
He began the Human
Genome Project at MIT

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:31.520
and is director of
the Human Genome

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:35.870
Center, which is affiliated
with the Whitehead Institute.

00:01:35.870 --> 00:01:37.790
The second story
is the relationship

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:41.300
between the Whitehead
Institute and MIT.

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:45.050
This relationship, which began
in a highly debated experiment

00:01:45.050 --> 00:01:50.030
some 15 years ago, has been
an unqualified success.

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:52.850
The Whitehead Institute
has produced great science,

00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:55.280
some of which we
celebrate today,

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:58.010
has produced great
leaders in science,

00:01:58.010 --> 00:02:02.420
and has played a full
role in education at MIT,

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:05.570
as typified by Eric's
outstanding lecturing

00:02:05.570 --> 00:02:09.199
in the Core Biology
course here in 10.250,

00:02:09.199 --> 00:02:11.990
which he does in the fall.

00:02:11.990 --> 00:02:15.770
Finally, there is the story of
the human genome sequence, what

00:02:15.770 --> 00:02:18.800
it tells us about
our past and future,

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:22.610
how the sequence was determined
in an international effort,

00:02:22.610 --> 00:02:25.760
and what it will
mean for humanity.

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:28.700
This story will be told
by Eric, the scientist who

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:31.610
led the team that
contributed the largest

00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:34.700
portion of the sequence
to the public effort

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:37.910
and who has been
its best spokesman.

00:02:37.910 --> 00:02:40.730
MIT can be proud that
one of its faculty

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:43.430
has accomplished an
historical first--

00:02:43.430 --> 00:02:45.990
the sequencing of
the human genome.

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:47.228
Eric.

00:02:47.228 --> 00:02:53.650
[APPLAUSE]

00:03:00.090 --> 00:03:02.420
LANDER: Let me start by-- oh--

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:04.790
I have no voice, by the way.

00:03:04.790 --> 00:03:06.750
One of the casualties
of this week,

00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:08.300
which has been a
rather intense week,

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:10.850
is that my voice seems to
have been lost somewhere

00:03:10.850 --> 00:03:12.800
between here and Washington.

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:15.800
So those of you who are
used to my lecturing in 701

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:18.550
may detect a somewhat more
painful version of it today.

00:03:18.550 --> 00:03:20.300
But, in any case, let
me correct one thing

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:21.841
Phil said right at
the end, because I

00:03:21.841 --> 00:03:24.380
think it's very important.

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:26.900
One of its faculty
members has completed

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:28.520
the sequencing of the genome.

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:30.410
Of course-- and I
know Phil means it--

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:34.670
what happened is, about
250 people here at MIT

00:03:34.670 --> 00:03:37.610
and the Whitehead
Institute have completed

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:40.580
this remarkable project of
sequencing the human genome.

00:03:40.580 --> 00:03:42.650
Because I think one
of the really most

00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:46.160
important aspects
of this project

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:48.200
is that nobody
can do this alone.

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:53.720
This was the first example of
a truly, truly collaborative

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:56.169
effort of enormous
proportions in biology.

00:03:56.169 --> 00:03:58.460
And I don't think it will be
the last of those efforts.

00:03:58.460 --> 00:04:01.640
I think we've learned
that there are projects

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:03.110
that we need to
get done to provide

00:04:03.110 --> 00:04:05.840
the infrastructure
for our field,

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:09.610
to provide the tools that the
single, smart graduate student

00:04:09.610 --> 00:04:13.250
and postdoc can use for
her or his experiments.

00:04:13.250 --> 00:04:16.910
And that we need to work
together, to make those tools.

00:04:16.910 --> 00:04:19.250
And I think, before
I go anywhere,

00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:22.252
I want to say how tremendously,
tremendously honored

00:04:22.252 --> 00:04:23.960
and pleased and proud
I am to have gotten

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:27.260
to work with so many
people here, together,

00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:29.840
in this community,
to get this job done.

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:31.520
And any one of us
had a small part,

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:34.970
but what I can say definitely
is, if we here at MIT

00:04:34.970 --> 00:04:37.040
had not pulled
together to do this

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:38.850
it wouldn't have gotten done.

00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:40.530
And that's a pretty good thing.

00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:43.010
If not any one of us
was essential to us,

00:04:43.010 --> 00:04:46.440
this community was
absolutely essential to it.

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:49.010
So this is very much
a shared talked,

00:04:49.010 --> 00:04:51.320
but I'm very honored to talk
about it on behalf of all

00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:54.492
the people here who did it.

00:04:54.492 --> 00:04:55.950
I'm looking out
upon the community.

00:04:55.950 --> 00:04:58.940
I see members of the genome
center here, which is great.

00:04:58.940 --> 00:05:01.964
I see 701 students here,
some of my 701 students,

00:05:01.964 --> 00:05:03.380
because I lecture
here in the fall

00:05:03.380 --> 00:05:05.090
and I'm glad to
see you back again.

00:05:05.090 --> 00:05:07.340
And many of my colleagues
in the Biology department.

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:08.150
And then there are
probably are a whole lot

00:05:08.150 --> 00:05:09.710
of people who are not
biologists at all who

00:05:09.710 --> 00:05:12.170
just came because they were
to find out what in the world

00:05:12.170 --> 00:05:13.700
was going on with the
Human Genome Project.

00:05:13.700 --> 00:05:15.949
And I'm going to try to be
able to speak appropriately

00:05:15.949 --> 00:05:21.060
to everybody and just talk a
little bit about what went on,

00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:25.500
what we learned from it,
and where it's all going.

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:27.510
Let me just dive right in.

00:05:27.510 --> 00:05:31.260
The Human Genome
Project and Beyond.

00:05:31.260 --> 00:05:35.160
The Human Genome Project was
an absolutely ludicrous notion,

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:37.830
proposed in about 1985--

00:05:37.830 --> 00:05:42.660
maybe '84, '85-- that we could
determine the entire sequence

00:05:42.660 --> 00:05:44.580
of the human genome
and that this

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:48.210
would provide incredibly useful
information for all of biology.

00:05:48.210 --> 00:05:50.820
Both of those propositions were
open to tremendous challenge--

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:53.010
the first, that
we could do this.

00:05:53.010 --> 00:05:57.960
At the time, DNA sequencing
was less than a decade old.

00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:00.870
DNA sequencing was practiced
with radioactive slab

00:06:00.870 --> 00:06:04.980
gels held up against
film, to detect sequences.

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:07.140
It was stunningly inefficient.

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:09.180
It was not that long
before that point

00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:11.670
that people got
whole graduate theses

00:06:11.670 --> 00:06:14.280
for sequencing of a
couple of thousand letters

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:15.600
in some plasmid.

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:18.630
And here the field was talking
about a million times more

00:06:18.630 --> 00:06:20.164
sequencing than that.

00:06:20.164 --> 00:06:22.080
In addition, it was hotly
debated whether this

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:23.680
was worth anything at all.

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:26.080
Everyone-- not "every"--
many people said, well,

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:28.080
what would you do with
that information, anyway?

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:29.560
Who needs it.

00:06:29.560 --> 00:06:33.180
And the truth was, with regard
to both of those propositions,

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:34.800
the critics were right.

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:36.840
We had no clue how in
the world to do this.

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:39.840
If anybody had gotten up in
front of Congress and said,

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:41.670
we know how to sequence
the human genome,

00:06:41.670 --> 00:06:44.139
they would have been
guilty of perjury.

00:06:44.139 --> 00:06:45.930
Thank god nobody had
to say that under oath

00:06:45.930 --> 00:06:47.490
to get this project started.

00:06:47.490 --> 00:06:49.406
And if anyone said, we
know exactly what we're

00:06:49.406 --> 00:06:51.070
going to do with
the information,

00:06:51.070 --> 00:06:53.110
they would have been
lying to you, too.

00:06:53.110 --> 00:06:55.380
But what happened was
a marvelous process

00:06:55.380 --> 00:06:57.130
of scientific progress,
in the latter part

00:06:57.130 --> 00:06:58.870
of the '80s, when a number
of different committees

00:06:58.870 --> 00:07:00.328
and a number of
different countries

00:07:00.328 --> 00:07:01.960
and a number of
different discussions

00:07:01.960 --> 00:07:05.110
got together and refined this
project from what it was first,

00:07:05.110 --> 00:07:09.130
some megaproject,
into a number of steps

00:07:09.130 --> 00:07:11.890
that would produce intermediate
results that would immediately

00:07:11.890 --> 00:07:14.920
have feedback to science and
would involve dozens and dozens

00:07:14.920 --> 00:07:16.502
of groups around the world.

00:07:16.502 --> 00:07:18.460
This is just a timeline
of some of the events--

00:07:18.460 --> 00:07:20.230
with a mammalian-centric
point of view,

00:07:20.230 --> 00:07:23.080
because we here at MIT
and Whitehead have taken

00:07:23.080 --> 00:07:25.330
a mammalian-centric
point of view--

00:07:25.330 --> 00:07:28.440
of building the genetic maps
that let you trace inheritance.

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:30.190
That was the first
thing-- just landmarks.

00:07:30.190 --> 00:07:31.780
Not so many of
them, a few thousand

00:07:31.780 --> 00:07:33.970
that would let you trace
inheritance and families,

00:07:33.970 --> 00:07:35.440
making those maps
better and better

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:37.398
through three different
generations, shown here

00:07:37.398 --> 00:07:38.260
in colors.

00:07:38.260 --> 00:07:40.510
Then, once you found
by tracing inheritance

00:07:40.510 --> 00:07:42.940
that there's a gene in
a particular region,

00:07:42.940 --> 00:07:46.030
you would get the piece
of DNA by a physical map

00:07:46.030 --> 00:07:47.050
from that region.

00:07:47.050 --> 00:07:49.540
And you would study it, to
try to find the disease gene.

00:07:49.540 --> 00:07:51.123
But then, of course,
what you'd really

00:07:51.123 --> 00:07:53.166
like to do is sequence
all those pieces of DNA,

00:07:53.166 --> 00:07:55.540
so that, instead of having to
do lots of laboratory work,

00:07:55.540 --> 00:07:57.748
you would just double-click
with your computer and up

00:07:57.748 --> 00:07:59.500
would pop what you wanted.

00:07:59.500 --> 00:08:02.890
That, of course, being the
dream of biology experiments.

00:08:02.890 --> 00:08:07.600
In 1985-- sorry, 1996--

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:10.972
began a pilot project for
the sequencing effort, when

00:08:10.972 --> 00:08:12.430
a number of groups
around the world

00:08:12.430 --> 00:08:14.179
got together and said
we'd try to work out

00:08:14.179 --> 00:08:18.070
the methodologies for doing
large-scale sequencing,

00:08:18.070 --> 00:08:21.280
being able to do the
process at high quality,

00:08:21.280 --> 00:08:24.070
produce finished sequence
at the end, to automate it.

00:08:24.070 --> 00:08:27.100
And this was designated as about
a three-year pilot project.

00:08:27.100 --> 00:08:28.720
And that is indeed
what happened.

00:08:28.720 --> 00:08:33.020
In the spring of 1999, there
was a dramatic scale-up,

00:08:33.020 --> 00:08:34.900
because we entered
the production phase.

00:08:34.900 --> 00:08:37.659
And, over the course
of the next year or so,

00:08:37.659 --> 00:08:41.539
the vast majority of the
sequence got covered.

00:08:41.539 --> 00:08:44.240
It involved a tremendous
change in technology.

00:08:44.240 --> 00:08:48.260
When we began, in borrowed
space in the cancer center,

00:08:48.260 --> 00:08:51.760
we were using
single-channel pipetters.

00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:54.900
The great advance following that
was eight-channel pipetters.

00:08:54.900 --> 00:08:56.280
[LAUGHTER]

00:08:56.280 --> 00:08:59.360
Some favored the
12-channel pipetter.

00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:01.640
But, over the course
of the decade or so,

00:09:01.640 --> 00:09:03.410
we advanced and
advanced to the point

00:09:03.410 --> 00:09:06.560
where now the genome center
over at the Whitehead Institute

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:08.520
is filled with
robots of this sort.

00:09:08.520 --> 00:09:11.090
There are picking robots
that go around with cameras,

00:09:11.090 --> 00:09:13.430
picking bacterial clones
that had their picks

00:09:13.430 --> 00:09:16.325
96 colonies, dunks it
into grow, it picks 96.

00:09:16.325 --> 00:09:20.210
And, in the course of a
day, we pick about 120,000

00:09:20.210 --> 00:09:21.740
bacterial colonies.

00:09:21.740 --> 00:09:23.480
We then have to grow
them up overnight,

00:09:23.480 --> 00:09:25.190
and we have to purify them.

00:09:25.190 --> 00:09:28.460
Many of you in the audience
have done plasmid preps before,

00:09:28.460 --> 00:09:30.290
and then set up
sequencing reactions.

00:09:30.290 --> 00:09:32.120
It's a tedious operation.

00:09:32.120 --> 00:09:33.950
This is how we do them now.

00:09:33.950 --> 00:09:37.640
This is the production
floor for doing plasma preps

00:09:37.640 --> 00:09:39.200
and sequencing reactions.

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:40.790
Onto those conveyor belts--

00:09:40.790 --> 00:09:42.780
which use a new kind
of biochemistry,

00:09:42.780 --> 00:09:45.150
a solid-phase reversible
mobilization that was developed

00:09:45.150 --> 00:09:47.030
at the genome center.

00:09:47.030 --> 00:09:50.780
Onto these conveyor belts
go Microtiter plates

00:09:50.780 --> 00:09:52.479
of bacterial growths.

00:09:52.479 --> 00:09:54.520
They get cracked open,
the DNA gets precipitated,

00:09:54.520 --> 00:09:56.630
it gets washed and cleaned
in all different ways,

00:09:56.630 --> 00:09:59.210
reactions get set up,
and it chugs along

00:09:59.210 --> 00:10:01.190
at a pulse rate
of one Microtiter

00:10:01.190 --> 00:10:04.520
plate, about 96 clones per
minute, through the system,

00:10:04.520 --> 00:10:07.850
processing 120,000 clones
in the course of a day.

00:10:07.850 --> 00:10:11.210
You see a tremendous number
of machines in this picture.

00:10:11.210 --> 00:10:12.680
You see only a
handful of people.

00:10:12.680 --> 00:10:14.270
And that is correct.

00:10:14.270 --> 00:10:17.120
The remarkable development team
over at the genome center that

00:10:17.120 --> 00:10:20.866
built this robotics, in
conjunction with the company,

00:10:20.866 --> 00:10:23.240
in fact is significantly larger
than the number of people

00:10:23.240 --> 00:10:25.070
it takes to run
the robotics, which

00:10:25.070 --> 00:10:28.424
is really quite delightful.

00:10:28.424 --> 00:10:30.590
Once all the sequence
sequencing reactions come off,

00:10:30.590 --> 00:10:32.390
we have to detect them.

00:10:32.390 --> 00:10:35.090
And they go back to a room
of commercial detectors,

00:10:35.090 --> 00:10:36.320
back in the back room.

00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:40.280
We have a farm of about 145
capillary-based sequencers,

00:10:40.280 --> 00:10:45.030
with a throughput of about 65
million letters of DNA per day.

00:10:45.030 --> 00:10:47.630
That's a lot of
sequence per day.

00:10:47.630 --> 00:10:49.270
And then all of
that goes upstairs

00:10:49.270 --> 00:10:51.020
to the informatics
department, where folks

00:10:51.020 --> 00:10:53.186
process this and assemble
this and extract something

00:10:53.186 --> 00:10:55.280
from all the sequence.

00:10:55.280 --> 00:10:57.530
It was a very interesting
year, from May

00:10:57.530 --> 00:11:01.850
of 1999 to May of 2000, when the
amount of the genome that had

00:11:01.850 --> 00:11:03.850
been covered in draft form--

00:11:03.850 --> 00:11:06.320
not perfect form,
but draft form--

00:11:06.320 --> 00:11:09.770
had grown, grew from about
10% of the genome to about 90%

00:11:09.770 --> 00:11:13.340
of the genome, in the course
of one quite remarkable year.

00:11:13.340 --> 00:11:16.190
This is roughly what the
year looked like, here.

00:11:16.190 --> 00:11:18.020
It was month by
month, the genome.

00:11:18.020 --> 00:11:20.460
And I could just look at this
again and again and again.

00:11:20.460 --> 00:11:20.960
It just--

00:11:20.960 --> 00:11:21.380
[LAUGHTER]

00:11:21.380 --> 00:11:22.720
Really, I mean, let's
look at that again.

00:11:22.720 --> 00:11:23.345
That's really--

00:11:23.345 --> 00:11:24.145
[LAUGHTER]

00:11:24.145 --> 00:11:27.900
That's really fun.

00:11:27.900 --> 00:11:28.490
There you go!

00:11:28.490 --> 00:11:30.281
That's all there is to
sequencing a genome.

00:11:30.281 --> 00:11:32.030
No, this involved
a tremendous amount

00:11:32.030 --> 00:11:34.670
of coordination of 20 different
groups around the world,

00:11:34.670 --> 00:11:38.730
distributing clones, groups
working in all different sizes.

00:11:38.730 --> 00:11:40.730
Our group at the
Whitehead Institute

00:11:40.730 --> 00:11:43.910
was indeed the largest single
contributor to the effort,

00:11:43.910 --> 00:11:47.264
but in fact there were
major, major contributions

00:11:47.264 --> 00:11:48.680
from the Sanger
Centre in England,

00:11:48.680 --> 00:11:50.870
from Washington
University in St. Louis,

00:11:50.870 --> 00:11:53.274
from the Department of
Energy, from Baylor in Texas.

00:11:53.274 --> 00:11:55.190
And then a number of
small groups, all of whom

00:11:55.190 --> 00:11:57.650
played, I think, incredibly
important roles in keeping

00:11:57.650 --> 00:11:59.310
this an international project.

00:11:59.310 --> 00:12:02.690
In fact, the project was
so open that, halfway

00:12:02.690 --> 00:12:06.170
through its scale-up phase,
the group in Beijing, China,

00:12:06.170 --> 00:12:08.820
said they wanted to
join and we said, sure.

00:12:08.820 --> 00:12:11.310
And Beijing brought in 1
and 1/2% of the genome,

00:12:11.310 --> 00:12:12.532
done there in China.

00:12:12.532 --> 00:12:14.240
The groups were all
very different in how

00:12:14.240 --> 00:12:15.656
they approached
this, but they all

00:12:15.656 --> 00:12:18.380
shared one absolute
commitment, which

00:12:18.380 --> 00:12:20.870
was that the data,
however it was produced,

00:12:20.870 --> 00:12:22.550
would be freely
released on the web

00:12:22.550 --> 00:12:25.750
every 24 hours, without
restrictions of any kind.

00:12:25.750 --> 00:12:27.500
That was the only
condition for signing up

00:12:27.500 --> 00:12:31.440
to join the International
Human Genome Project.

00:12:31.440 --> 00:12:33.700
It was a pleasure to work
with all these people,

00:12:33.700 --> 00:12:36.120
and it was a pleasure
to do all this work.

00:12:36.120 --> 00:12:38.389
And I've got to say, there's
just absolutely nothing

00:12:38.389 --> 00:12:39.930
like just being able
to be left alone

00:12:39.930 --> 00:12:41.670
and focus on your science.

00:12:41.670 --> 00:12:44.027
And (EMPHASIS) this
was absolutely nothing

00:12:44.027 --> 00:12:46.485
like being able to be left
alone and focus on your science!

00:12:46.485 --> 00:12:48.720
[LAUGHTER]

00:12:48.720 --> 00:12:50.850
We spent the entire
year, while we're

00:12:50.850 --> 00:12:53.550
trying to sequence the
genome, waking up to newspaper

00:12:53.550 --> 00:12:54.930
headlines all the time.

00:12:54.930 --> 00:12:56.850
Because apparently,
I understand,

00:12:56.850 --> 00:12:59.766
some company decided that
we were being slow about it.

00:12:59.766 --> 00:13:01.590
[LAUGHTER]

00:13:01.590 --> 00:13:05.164
And decided to create
a business plan,

00:13:05.164 --> 00:13:07.830
that they were going to sequence
the human genome so much faster

00:13:07.830 --> 00:13:08.874
than anybody else.

00:13:08.874 --> 00:13:11.040
And they would therefore
have a proprietary database

00:13:11.040 --> 00:13:12.623
that they could do
with as they chose.

00:13:15.170 --> 00:13:18.710
To my mind, there was a
little miscalculation, there,

00:13:18.710 --> 00:13:22.970
in that I think they mistook
what was the designated pilot

00:13:22.970 --> 00:13:25.070
phase, where we were
attempting to figure out

00:13:25.070 --> 00:13:27.110
how to do this thing
carefully and right,

00:13:27.110 --> 00:13:29.920
for an inability to scale
up when we were ready.

00:13:29.920 --> 00:13:30.800
And so, in fact--

00:13:30.800 --> 00:13:32.940
I think somewhat
to their surprise--

00:13:32.940 --> 00:13:36.200
they found that we were zooming
along just fine, doing this,

00:13:36.200 --> 00:13:39.410
and that the business
plan which involved

00:13:39.410 --> 00:13:43.180
having three-year window where
the sequence was exclusively

00:13:43.180 --> 00:13:46.700
available through this
company really seemed to, um,

00:13:46.700 --> 00:13:48.860
well, it didn't look
so good, after a while.

00:13:48.860 --> 00:13:50.155
We'll come back to
what actually happened,

00:13:50.155 --> 00:13:51.080
because, scientifically,
there was

00:13:51.080 --> 00:13:52.310
a lot of very
interesting stuff there.

00:13:52.310 --> 00:13:53.851
There were some
claims that they were

00:13:53.851 --> 00:13:56.060
going to do this by a
much more powerful method,

00:13:56.060 --> 00:13:59.480
the much-vaunted
whole-genome-shotgun method,

00:13:59.480 --> 00:14:02.080
that, instead of dividing
the genome up into pieces,

00:14:02.080 --> 00:14:04.940
they were going to toss the
whole genome into your blender,

00:14:04.940 --> 00:14:09.007
press, you know, Puree, and be
able to sequence lots of pieces

00:14:09.007 --> 00:14:09.840
and put it together.

00:14:09.840 --> 00:14:12.374
And this was so much faster
than the silly old, you know,

00:14:12.374 --> 00:14:14.540
know where you are and get
the whole thing organized

00:14:14.540 --> 00:14:15.380
kind of way.

00:14:15.380 --> 00:14:15.980
[LAUGHTER]

00:14:15.980 --> 00:14:17.660
We're going to come
back to that point,

00:14:17.660 --> 00:14:19.951
because, for the first time,
we know the answer of what

00:14:19.951 --> 00:14:21.020
actually happened.

00:14:21.020 --> 00:14:24.470
But, given all the controversy
of what was going on,

00:14:24.470 --> 00:14:26.870
we decided that the
best thing to do--

00:14:26.870 --> 00:14:29.810
I believe Senator Aiken really
gets credit for this strategy--

00:14:29.810 --> 00:14:31.770
was simply to declare victory.

00:14:31.770 --> 00:14:32.270
Right?

00:14:32.270 --> 00:14:34.010
This was the old
Vietnam strategy, right?

00:14:34.010 --> 00:14:35.420
[LAUGHTER]

00:14:35.420 --> 00:14:37.490
In his case, it was declare
victory and withdraw.

00:14:37.490 --> 00:14:39.650
We just declared victory
and went back to our labs

00:14:39.650 --> 00:14:41.840
to get to work, you know,
finishing the genome.

00:14:41.840 --> 00:14:45.110
Back on June 26, there was a big
announcement at the White House

00:14:45.110 --> 00:14:47.660
that the human genome
had been sequenced.

00:14:47.660 --> 00:14:49.970
Um-- at least, if
you read closely,

00:14:49.970 --> 00:14:51.680
that a draft version
of the human genome

00:14:51.680 --> 00:14:52.820
had been sequenced.

00:14:52.820 --> 00:14:55.250
And, if you read really
closely, that the groups

00:14:55.250 --> 00:14:57.380
claim that a draft
sequence of the--

00:14:57.380 --> 00:15:00.260
[LAUGHTER]

00:15:00.260 --> 00:15:02.720
Obviously, in our case, you
could check the web yourself

00:15:02.720 --> 00:15:03.790
and see that there
was data there,

00:15:03.790 --> 00:15:04.950
or that you would have
to analyze it yourself.

00:15:04.950 --> 00:15:06.586
The case of the
company, you know,

00:15:06.586 --> 00:15:08.210
go figure-- how would
you know, anyway?

00:15:08.210 --> 00:15:09.690
But it didn't stop
the announcements,

00:15:09.690 --> 00:15:10.820
and it was basically right.

00:15:10.820 --> 00:15:14.070
Because there had been an
amazing milestone achieved,

00:15:14.070 --> 00:15:15.770
which was that it was
no longer the case

00:15:15.770 --> 00:15:19.627
that the lack of sequence was a
rate-limiting step for biology,

00:15:19.627 --> 00:15:21.710
that, for the vast majority
regions of the genome,

00:15:21.710 --> 00:15:24.290
you could get your
hands on the sequence.

00:15:24.290 --> 00:15:26.810
But what we hadn't
done is analyze

00:15:26.810 --> 00:15:29.060
that sequence, put it
together, neatly seen

00:15:29.060 --> 00:15:31.580
what's in it, really
read through the book.

00:15:31.580 --> 00:15:34.250
And, to my mind-- although I
realize it seems sort of pokey

00:15:34.250 --> 00:15:37.100
and old-fashioned-- instead of
science by press release, to me

00:15:37.100 --> 00:15:39.740
the real milestones come
with peer-reviewed papers.

00:15:39.740 --> 00:15:42.060
And that's what in fact
happened this week.

00:15:42.060 --> 00:15:43.700
That's what was
exciting, this week,

00:15:43.700 --> 00:15:47.550
was that a paper appeared
in the journal Nature.

00:15:47.550 --> 00:15:50.690
In fact, a remarkable collection
of about 20 papers appeared

00:15:50.690 --> 00:15:54.740
in the journal Nature, one
of which [? cub-reports ?]

00:15:54.740 --> 00:15:57.530
the sequence of the human
genome in an initial analysis.

00:15:57.530 --> 00:16:00.456
Some others report other
related topics I'll talk about.

00:16:00.456 --> 00:16:02.330
It appeared officially,
covered it yesterday.

00:16:02.330 --> 00:16:05.090
We held a press conference
on Monday, to announce this.

00:16:05.090 --> 00:16:07.255
And this picture
here, by the way,

00:16:07.255 --> 00:16:09.380
is one we designed here at
the Whitehead Institute.

00:16:09.380 --> 00:16:10.963
You may not be able
to see it closely,

00:16:10.963 --> 00:16:12.560
but this is a
picture of DNA that

00:16:12.560 --> 00:16:16.350
has been decomposed through
the photo-mosaic technique

00:16:16.350 --> 00:16:19.425
into 1,500 human faces
from around the world.

00:16:19.425 --> 00:16:21.800
And so, when you get your
issue of Nature, look up close.

00:16:21.800 --> 00:16:23.285
We did this here,
and-- in fact, we

00:16:23.285 --> 00:16:24.660
had this idea of
doing this here,

00:16:24.660 --> 00:16:27.410
and then we searched the web
to find out where in the world

00:16:27.410 --> 00:16:30.260
was that company that
made these photo mosaics.

00:16:30.260 --> 00:16:32.720
And it was absolutely
delightful to discover

00:16:32.720 --> 00:16:35.120
that this company that I'd
heard of but never really

00:16:35.120 --> 00:16:38.180
paid enough attention to was
the product of an MIT graduate

00:16:38.180 --> 00:16:39.950
and was, in fact,
two blocks down, just

00:16:39.950 --> 00:16:41.260
by the Necco factory.

00:16:41.260 --> 00:16:43.900
[APPLAUSE]

00:16:43.900 --> 00:16:46.400
And so MIT pops up
all over the place.

00:16:46.400 --> 00:16:48.070
You know, it was great.

00:16:48.070 --> 00:16:51.654
Anyway, for fun, go try to find
the picture of Crick and Watson

00:16:51.654 --> 00:16:52.320
in this picture.

00:16:52.320 --> 00:16:53.790
[LAUGHTER]

00:16:53.790 --> 00:16:55.501
There is a Where's
Waldo to this.

00:16:55.501 --> 00:16:56.000
So--

00:16:56.000 --> 00:16:57.540
[LAUGHTER]

00:16:57.540 --> 00:16:58.040
It's there.

00:17:00.195 --> 00:17:02.570
All right, so, a little bit
about assembling the sequence

00:17:02.570 --> 00:17:03.230
of the human genome.

00:17:03.230 --> 00:17:05.300
I realize many of you aren't
aficionados for this stuff.

00:17:05.300 --> 00:17:07.520
I think I'm scheduled to give
a proper seminar in April.

00:17:07.520 --> 00:17:09.478
This, as you can tell,
is not a proper seminar.

00:17:09.478 --> 00:17:11.986
This is kind of fun to
celebrate this week.

00:17:11.986 --> 00:17:13.819
Assembling the sequence
of the human genome.

00:17:13.819 --> 00:17:16.040
Very briefly, we
had to produce--

00:17:16.040 --> 00:17:17.028
and we did produce--

00:17:17.028 --> 00:17:19.069
this is largely the work
of Bob Waterston's group

00:17:19.069 --> 00:17:19.819
in St. Louis--

00:17:19.819 --> 00:17:22.640
overlapping clone maps,
across the genome,

00:17:22.640 --> 00:17:25.819
of large inserts clones,
each about 200,000 letters

00:17:25.819 --> 00:17:29.720
in length, and then to select
from that a reasonable tiling

00:17:29.720 --> 00:17:32.030
path of these large,
we called them BACs--

00:17:32.030 --> 00:17:34.280
Bacterial Artificial
Chromosomes-- these large,

00:17:34.280 --> 00:17:38.690
200,000-base-pair clones,
break up each of these things

00:17:38.690 --> 00:17:42.530
into small, shotgun
fragments, sequence them,

00:17:42.530 --> 00:17:46.220
and reassemble them into a draft
form that still had some gaps

00:17:46.220 --> 00:17:47.840
but a modest number of gaps--

00:17:47.840 --> 00:17:50.096
perhaps eight, nine gaps,
something like that.

00:17:50.096 --> 00:17:51.470
And then, with
computer programs,

00:17:51.470 --> 00:17:55.220
merge them all together
into overlapping sequences,

00:17:55.220 --> 00:17:57.470
based on where we knew
these clones were.

00:17:57.470 --> 00:18:01.160
And there's a very nice program,
written by a graduate student

00:18:01.160 --> 00:18:05.270
at UC Santa Cruz, called
Jim Kent, called a "gig

00:18:05.270 --> 00:18:07.310
assembler" that put all
these fragments together

00:18:07.310 --> 00:18:08.736
in some nice way.

00:18:08.736 --> 00:18:10.610
And the short version
of this is that there's

00:18:10.610 --> 00:18:12.440
a fair amount of continuity.

00:18:12.440 --> 00:18:15.710
You start initially with
about 22 kilobase contigs

00:18:15.710 --> 00:18:16.820
of sequence.

00:18:16.820 --> 00:18:19.280
Merging them, you get them
about 82 kilobase contigs.

00:18:19.280 --> 00:18:21.530
Connecting them by links,
you're up to about a quarter

00:18:21.530 --> 00:18:23.030
of a million bases.

00:18:23.030 --> 00:18:25.850
They live within
larger clone contigs

00:18:25.850 --> 00:18:28.640
that we have localized
all these fragments into,

00:18:28.640 --> 00:18:31.010
that extend for more
than 2 million bases

00:18:31.010 --> 00:18:33.500
and connect it to
about 5 million bases

00:18:33.500 --> 00:18:35.540
and then are joined by
fingerprints into about 8

00:18:35.540 --> 00:18:36.800
million bases.

00:18:36.800 --> 00:18:38.930
And so, in fact, there's
a hierarchical arrangement

00:18:38.930 --> 00:18:41.100
of each of these
fragments connected in.

00:18:41.100 --> 00:18:43.070
There are still gaps
throughout this map,

00:18:43.070 --> 00:18:45.320
but we know where the vast
majority of those gaps are.

00:18:45.320 --> 00:18:48.860
In fact, almost all of those
gaps, 99% of the sequence,

00:18:48.860 --> 00:18:53.280
is well localized to the human
chromosomes by this process.

00:18:53.280 --> 00:18:54.300
It ain't perfect.

00:18:54.300 --> 00:18:55.297
It ain't finished.

00:18:55.297 --> 00:18:56.880
But, for every letter
in the sequence,

00:18:56.880 --> 00:18:59.004
we've attached an error rate.

00:18:59.004 --> 00:19:00.420
91% of the letters
in the sequence

00:19:00.420 --> 00:19:03.240
are accurate to 99.99%.

00:19:03.240 --> 00:19:07.000
95% are accurate to 99.9%.

00:19:07.000 --> 00:19:07.614
The order?

00:19:07.614 --> 00:19:09.780
Well, we've checked it
against all the previous maps

00:19:09.780 --> 00:19:10.650
available.

00:19:10.650 --> 00:19:12.810
It agrees pretty darn
well, although there

00:19:12.810 --> 00:19:15.090
are the occasional
differences in the order.

00:19:15.090 --> 00:19:16.740
Where we've checked
those differences,

00:19:16.740 --> 00:19:18.330
so far they appear
to be problems

00:19:18.330 --> 00:19:19.530
with the previous maps.

00:19:19.530 --> 00:19:20.905
In fact, the
sequence seems to do

00:19:20.905 --> 00:19:23.029
a pretty good job of cleaning
up the previous maps,

00:19:23.029 --> 00:19:24.130
rather than vice versa.

00:19:24.130 --> 00:19:27.132
But by no means will we assert
that any of this is perfect.

00:19:27.132 --> 00:19:28.590
There's still
probably about a year

00:19:28.590 --> 00:19:30.780
and a half's more work to
go to bring this to what

00:19:30.780 --> 00:19:32.490
we call "finished state."

00:19:32.490 --> 00:19:35.940
But, already, there's an
awful lot of genome there.

00:19:35.940 --> 00:19:40.020
The connected pieces of
sequence, the sequences

00:19:40.020 --> 00:19:41.610
are connected into scaffolds.

00:19:41.610 --> 00:19:47.010
And together they span
about 2.892 billion bases,

00:19:47.010 --> 00:19:50.130
which appears to be about the
entire euchromatic portion

00:19:50.130 --> 00:19:51.780
of the genome.

00:19:51.780 --> 00:19:54.270
The genome's probably
about 3.1 billion bases,

00:19:54.270 --> 00:19:56.040
the rest of it being
heterochromatin

00:19:56.040 --> 00:19:58.260
around centromeres
and other regions.

00:19:58.260 --> 00:20:02.150
Now, of the 2.92, we
seem to have about 2.69

00:20:02.150 --> 00:20:04.110
in our assembly.

00:20:04.110 --> 00:20:07.050
That's about 92% of the
genome seems to be represented

00:20:07.050 --> 00:20:09.030
in our assembled bases.

00:20:09.030 --> 00:20:09.870
There are gaps.

00:20:09.870 --> 00:20:12.990
There's about 149,000
gaps in the sequence.

00:20:12.990 --> 00:20:17.740
But all but 2,000 of them
we have immediately spanned

00:20:17.740 --> 00:20:19.969
by pieces, because they
live well within clones,

00:20:19.969 --> 00:20:22.260
and they are all going back
through a finishing process

00:20:22.260 --> 00:20:23.610
and getting closed up.

00:20:23.610 --> 00:20:26.820
Because of the wonderful
mapping approach that was taken,

00:20:26.820 --> 00:20:30.360
all these pieces happen to
live within a mere 2,000

00:20:30.360 --> 00:20:33.330
components that have
to be put onto the map.

00:20:33.330 --> 00:20:36.150
And the number of physical
gaps, where we don't actually

00:20:36.150 --> 00:20:39.660
own a piece of DNA covering
it right now, is under 1,000.

00:20:39.660 --> 00:20:41.640
Those are being worked through.

00:20:41.640 --> 00:20:43.170
So that's a rough description.

00:20:43.170 --> 00:20:45.750
In any case, this is all
just to say it's not done.

00:20:45.750 --> 00:20:47.960
But it's pretty far along.

00:20:47.960 --> 00:20:49.335
It's pretty far along.

00:20:49.335 --> 00:20:51.353
Then there were our
friends at the company.

00:20:51.353 --> 00:20:52.340
[LAUGHTER]

00:20:52.340 --> 00:20:54.450
So I keep reading, in
the New York Times,

00:20:54.450 --> 00:20:57.650
how they beat us at this thing.

00:20:57.650 --> 00:20:59.460
It was really,
really fun this week.

00:20:59.460 --> 00:21:02.790
We got to read the paper.

00:21:02.790 --> 00:21:05.934
So they really did try their
whole genome-shotgun thing.

00:21:05.934 --> 00:21:07.350
They generated a
whole lot of data

00:21:07.350 --> 00:21:11.500
in house, about
fivefold coverage.

00:21:11.500 --> 00:21:14.289
They report no assembly
of it whatsoever.

00:21:14.289 --> 00:21:16.330
I assume, from the fact
that nowhere in the paper

00:21:16.330 --> 00:21:18.121
have they attempted to
assemble their data,

00:21:18.121 --> 00:21:19.940
that it didn't work at all.

00:21:19.940 --> 00:21:22.030
Instead what they did,
if you read the paper--

00:21:22.030 --> 00:21:24.830
and I invite my colleagues now
to read the paper closely--

00:21:24.830 --> 00:21:28.750
was they took their coverage,
and they took the whole public

00:21:28.750 --> 00:21:33.900
database, based on
seven-and-a-halffold coverage.

00:21:33.900 --> 00:21:36.480
They didn't even break it
up randomly or anything.

00:21:36.480 --> 00:21:38.910
They got out the screwdrivers,
and they carefully

00:21:38.910 --> 00:21:44.280
unscrewed it into a perfectly
overlapping, tiling path,

00:21:44.280 --> 00:21:47.190
with exactly equal,
big overlaps,

00:21:47.190 --> 00:21:51.690
tossed it into their computer,
and then added their data

00:21:51.690 --> 00:21:54.480
and attempted to
assemble a genome.

00:21:54.480 --> 00:21:58.350
60% of the data that went
into the computer was ours.

00:21:58.350 --> 00:22:02.100
It was not experimental
data, it was simulated data--

00:22:02.100 --> 00:22:04.260
simulated, not even as
you do in a laboratory,

00:22:04.260 --> 00:22:06.210
but with perfect overlaps.

00:22:06.210 --> 00:22:08.760
Wish I knew how to generate
data like that in a laboratory.

00:22:08.760 --> 00:22:13.140
Nonetheless, nonetheless,
the whole genome shotgun

00:22:13.140 --> 00:22:15.130
still failed.

00:22:15.130 --> 00:22:19.750
Despite all that, it covers
about 75% of the genome,

00:22:19.750 --> 00:22:21.940
in contigs that you
can really localize.

00:22:21.940 --> 00:22:24.719
If you ask, how much does
it cover, total, here's

00:22:24.719 --> 00:22:25.510
what it looks like.

00:22:25.510 --> 00:22:29.240
They call it-- the word "faux"
is their word, in the paper.

00:22:29.240 --> 00:22:34.270
Their faux whole genome
assembly actually has about 88%

00:22:34.270 --> 00:22:36.100
of the sequence
in it, although it

00:22:36.100 --> 00:22:40.360
is in 119,000 separate pieces
that have to be localized,

00:22:40.360 --> 00:22:41.860
far beyond which
you can localize.

00:22:41.860 --> 00:22:44.380
If you take the biggest
ones, you can get about 75%

00:22:44.380 --> 00:22:46.190
of the genome
realistically localized.

00:22:46.190 --> 00:22:49.300
And about 25% of
the genome is in,

00:22:49.300 --> 00:22:53.093
I guess the technical term
is, "tossed genome salad."

00:22:53.093 --> 00:22:55.020
[LAUGHTER]

00:22:55.020 --> 00:22:57.639
So the assembly that actually
got reported by our friends--

00:22:57.639 --> 00:22:59.680
although this makes no
difference to the New York

00:22:59.680 --> 00:23:02.442
Times-- was not that
whole genome assembly.

00:23:02.442 --> 00:23:04.150
What they did instead,
when they realized

00:23:04.150 --> 00:23:05.800
they had trouble with
this, was they simply said,

00:23:05.800 --> 00:23:07.490
all right, we'll
take all our data,

00:23:07.490 --> 00:23:10.300
we'll lay it on top
of the public clones,

00:23:10.300 --> 00:23:12.370
in a clone-based
assembly, and we'll just

00:23:12.370 --> 00:23:14.436
assemble the local bits.

00:23:14.436 --> 00:23:16.060
So, instead of a
whole genome assembly,

00:23:16.060 --> 00:23:20.110
they did assemblies on the order
of about half a bacterial log.

00:23:20.110 --> 00:23:22.870
That, however, did work.

00:23:22.870 --> 00:23:27.640
Of course, what it produced
was about 2.65 billion bases

00:23:27.640 --> 00:23:29.080
of DNA.

00:23:29.080 --> 00:23:31.990
The input to that, of course,
was our 2.69 billion bases

00:23:31.990 --> 00:23:33.616
of DNA.

00:23:33.616 --> 00:23:34.930
[LAUGHTER]

00:23:34.930 --> 00:23:37.840
And it produced about the
same coverage of the genome.

00:23:37.840 --> 00:23:40.319
So I mean, I don't mean
to make a big deal out

00:23:40.319 --> 00:23:41.110
of this difference.

00:23:41.110 --> 00:23:42.590
This is basically the same.

00:23:42.590 --> 00:23:44.944
So I think in fact
what they did was

00:23:44.944 --> 00:23:47.110
they rearranged our sentences
within our paragraphs,

00:23:47.110 --> 00:23:48.520
or something like that.

00:23:48.520 --> 00:23:51.280
I must say, after putting up
with three years of science

00:23:51.280 --> 00:23:53.440
by press conference,
it is a pleasure

00:23:53.440 --> 00:23:56.680
to read a scientific paper and
find out what really happened.

00:23:56.680 --> 00:23:59.740
And, I got to say, there
were some pretty dark times

00:23:59.740 --> 00:24:01.810
in the course of the
Human Genome Project,

00:24:01.810 --> 00:24:04.150
when we felt, should we
just throw in the towel?

00:24:04.150 --> 00:24:05.590
Was this really
possible to put up

00:24:05.590 --> 00:24:07.900
with a steady drumbeat
of press telling us

00:24:07.900 --> 00:24:10.900
that we're being silly for
being slow and wasteful

00:24:10.900 --> 00:24:12.550
and all these things?

00:24:12.550 --> 00:24:15.610
In the end, there would
be no Celera paper

00:24:15.610 --> 00:24:17.650
but for the Human
Genome Project.

00:24:17.650 --> 00:24:18.460
And you know what?

00:24:18.460 --> 00:24:20.460
We're very proud to have
put our data on the web

00:24:20.460 --> 00:24:22.180
and let them have our
data and all that.

00:24:22.180 --> 00:24:23.990
The only thing I ask
is that they probably

00:24:23.990 --> 00:24:27.130
ought to do a better job
of acknowledging, you know,

00:24:27.130 --> 00:24:28.461
why they have an assembly.

00:24:28.461 --> 00:24:29.960
But they're welcome
to the assembly!

00:24:29.960 --> 00:24:31.660
The data's there
for everybody, even

00:24:31.660 --> 00:24:34.480
for those who would like to say
that they're competing with us

00:24:34.480 --> 00:24:35.500
or beating us.

00:24:35.500 --> 00:24:38.320
That is the one great thing
about making your data freely

00:24:38.320 --> 00:24:41.380
available, is because you
have no chance of winning,

00:24:41.380 --> 00:24:43.390
because you give everything
to your competitors,

00:24:43.390 --> 00:24:46.559
you absolutely are guaranteed
to have no chance of losing.

00:24:46.559 --> 00:24:48.100
And that's important,
because we have

00:24:48.100 --> 00:24:50.620
to win by giving
this stuff away.

00:24:50.620 --> 00:24:51.220
All right.

00:24:51.220 --> 00:24:52.043
Inter--

00:24:52.043 --> 00:24:57.466
[APPLAUSE]

00:24:59.940 --> 00:25:01.344
It's a good lesson.

00:25:01.344 --> 00:25:03.010
Interpreting the
sequence of the genome.

00:25:03.010 --> 00:25:04.551
So I want to put
aside all that stuff

00:25:04.551 --> 00:25:07.000
and say, well, what's
in your genome, anyway?

00:25:07.000 --> 00:25:10.810
Well, a lot of people around
the world combined their efforts

00:25:10.810 --> 00:25:13.390
and put on all the different
annotations we know.

00:25:13.390 --> 00:25:16.110
There is a browser,
at the University

00:25:16.110 --> 00:25:18.497
of California, Santa
Cruz, where we display

00:25:18.497 --> 00:25:20.080
all the different
RNAs that are known,

00:25:20.080 --> 00:25:22.990
all the ESTs that are
known, all the similarities

00:25:22.990 --> 00:25:25.420
to the pufferfish, tetraodon,
and mouses going on

00:25:25.420 --> 00:25:27.175
in the next month or
two, all the repeat

00:25:27.175 --> 00:25:29.800
sequences, and anything else you
want to know about the genome.

00:25:29.800 --> 00:25:32.950
Either the folks at Santa Cruz--

00:25:32.950 --> 00:25:35.470
Here's the Santa Cruz
browser, if you visit it.

00:25:35.470 --> 00:25:39.020
You can zoom in or zoom out,
according to your preference.

00:25:39.020 --> 00:25:40.870
You can scroll along
the chromosome.

00:25:40.870 --> 00:25:43.270
You can set it for which
things you want to see,

00:25:43.270 --> 00:25:45.556
which things you
don't want to see.

00:25:45.556 --> 00:25:48.180
And, if you have any suggestions
for what else should be there,

00:25:48.180 --> 00:25:50.220
let us know and it'll get added.

00:25:50.220 --> 00:25:53.070
In addition, because we
believe in diversity,

00:25:53.070 --> 00:25:54.960
the European
Bioinformatics Institute

00:25:54.960 --> 00:25:56.580
has its own version
of this browser.

00:25:56.580 --> 00:25:59.410
Same sequence is used, here,
but they put it up differently.

00:25:59.410 --> 00:26:02.070
They have different approaches
to how they're annotating it.

00:26:02.070 --> 00:26:04.230
The National Center for
Biotechnology Information

00:26:04.230 --> 00:26:06.060
also is bringing up a browser.

00:26:06.060 --> 00:26:09.210
And there'll be a lot of diverse
ways to look at this sequence.

00:26:09.210 --> 00:26:11.870
There is also, when you get
your copy of Nature, this way

00:26:11.870 --> 00:26:12.870
to look at the sequence.

00:26:12.870 --> 00:26:16.080
We have a foldout-- in fact,
two roll-folds-- that come out

00:26:16.080 --> 00:26:19.320
of the journal, with the
human genome on it, showing

00:26:19.320 --> 00:26:22.812
the key features like
the density of Gs and Cs

00:26:22.812 --> 00:26:24.270
and SINE elements
and LINE elements

00:26:24.270 --> 00:26:26.715
and all sorts of things.

00:26:26.715 --> 00:26:29.400
It is, I must say, not at
a terribly usable scale.

00:26:29.400 --> 00:26:33.310
It's at a scale of 3.3 million
bases per centimeter, which

00:26:33.310 --> 00:26:33.810
is--

00:26:33.810 --> 00:26:35.130
[LAUGHTER] --beyond

00:26:35.130 --> 00:26:36.150
My visual acuity.

00:26:36.150 --> 00:26:37.860
But, in any case--

00:26:37.860 --> 00:26:40.757
but it looks pretty to
put up on your wall.

00:26:40.757 --> 00:26:42.340
But you notice certain
things from it.

00:26:42.340 --> 00:26:43.756
In fact, going
back to it, you can

00:26:43.756 --> 00:26:46.690
see, if your eye is really
good, that the GC content, which

00:26:46.690 --> 00:26:49.390
is this black line, goes
up and down a fair amount.

00:26:49.390 --> 00:26:52.450
The genes, there is some dense
urban neighborhoods of genes,

00:26:52.450 --> 00:26:55.900
some rural neighborhoods,
here, of genes.

00:26:55.900 --> 00:26:57.960
If you plot it, the
genome is really lumpy.

00:26:57.960 --> 00:26:59.920
We're very lumpy,
compared to other species.

00:26:59.920 --> 00:27:05.440
There are regions where
your GC content's 41%.

00:27:05.440 --> 00:27:08.920
There are regions where it takes
an excursion out by 10% or 15%,

00:27:08.920 --> 00:27:11.080
for periods of 10
million bases, before it

00:27:11.080 --> 00:27:12.520
comes back down again.

00:27:12.520 --> 00:27:15.040
And, you know, even a little
bit of statistics will tell you

00:27:15.040 --> 00:27:17.500
this can't possibly
happen by chance.

00:27:17.500 --> 00:27:19.500
In fact, there are very
distinct neighborhoods.

00:27:19.500 --> 00:27:22.840
The GC-rich regions
appear to be gene-rich.

00:27:22.840 --> 00:27:25.930
In fact, we have now great data
about how gene-rich they are.

00:27:25.930 --> 00:27:28.540
They also, by the way,
correlate to many other things

00:27:28.540 --> 00:27:30.340
such as chromosome
banding pattern.

00:27:30.340 --> 00:27:32.080
There's a very
strong correlation,

00:27:32.080 --> 00:27:35.390
where the GC-rich regions
correspond to the light bands

00:27:35.390 --> 00:27:36.232
in chromosomes.

00:27:36.232 --> 00:27:37.690
And, in one of the
companion papers

00:27:37.690 --> 00:27:40.520
to come out in yesterday's
issue of Nature,

00:27:40.520 --> 00:27:45.250
one of the consortium groups put
about 8,000 of the BAC clones

00:27:45.250 --> 00:27:47.860
that had been sequenced
onto the cytogenetic map

00:27:47.860 --> 00:27:49.419
and drew these
correspondences, which

00:27:49.419 --> 00:27:51.460
is useful both for the
general statement but also

00:27:51.460 --> 00:27:53.680
for, say, cancer
genetics, where one

00:27:53.680 --> 00:27:55.322
can take cytogenetic
rearrangements

00:27:55.322 --> 00:27:57.280
and correlate them now
to sequence, because you

00:27:57.280 --> 00:27:59.860
have 8,000 tide points.

00:27:59.860 --> 00:28:06.450
Now, you may know that most
of your genome is not genes.

00:28:06.450 --> 00:28:10.870
In fact, only about 1% to 1 and
1/2% of your genome is genes.

00:28:10.870 --> 00:28:12.950
Most of your genome
are repeat elements.

00:28:12.950 --> 00:28:14.710
They're repeat sequences.

00:28:14.710 --> 00:28:17.950
They are transposable elements
that have inhabited our genome

00:28:17.950 --> 00:28:20.410
and hopped around, over
the course of hundreds

00:28:20.410 --> 00:28:23.630
of millions of years.

00:28:23.630 --> 00:28:26.250
From their point of view, that's
the purpose of our genome,

00:28:26.250 --> 00:28:28.400
is to carry around them,
because they certainly

00:28:28.400 --> 00:28:31.690
are the majority occupant.

00:28:31.690 --> 00:28:35.120
They consider the genes
a necessary detail

00:28:35.120 --> 00:28:37.640
in order to propagate the
transposable elements and all

00:28:37.640 --> 00:28:38.139
that.

00:28:38.139 --> 00:28:42.560
But, when you go
back to 15 years ago,

00:28:42.560 --> 00:28:45.380
people said, ah, you know,
thee repeats, they're boring.

00:28:45.380 --> 00:28:47.960
I might have actually thought
that, too, even five years ago,

00:28:47.960 --> 00:28:50.870
but, I've got to say, they
are unbelievably interesting,

00:28:50.870 --> 00:28:52.190
these repeats.

00:28:52.190 --> 00:28:54.830
Very briefly, almost all
repeats in your genome

00:28:54.830 --> 00:28:56.490
fall into four flavors.

00:28:56.490 --> 00:28:59.810
One are called LINE elements,
one are called SINE elements,

00:28:59.810 --> 00:29:02.790
one are called LTR
retrovirus-like elements,

00:29:02.790 --> 00:29:06.014
one are called DNA transposons.

00:29:06.014 --> 00:29:07.430
For example, the
LINE elements are

00:29:07.430 --> 00:29:10.250
one of the great inventions
in the history of eukaryotes.

00:29:10.250 --> 00:29:12.500
They are a transposable element.

00:29:12.500 --> 00:29:14.730
They make an RNA that
goes off to the cytoplasm

00:29:14.730 --> 00:29:16.230
and [INAUDIBLE]
reading [? frames ?]

00:29:16.230 --> 00:29:18.010
it gets translated into protein.

00:29:18.010 --> 00:29:20.690
The protein grabs the RNA
that made it, brings it back

00:29:20.690 --> 00:29:23.600
to the nucleus, cuts the
chromosome at a site,

00:29:23.600 --> 00:29:26.400
and reverse-transcribes it
back into the chromosome.

00:29:26.400 --> 00:29:30.410
It's a completely self-contained
package for making an RNA,

00:29:30.410 --> 00:29:31.940
getting the
machinery translated,

00:29:31.940 --> 00:29:34.620
coming back to the nucleus,
and copying yourself.

00:29:34.620 --> 00:29:37.490
You'd think, the
ultimate parasite.

00:29:37.490 --> 00:29:39.380
SINE elements,
interestingly, are

00:29:39.380 --> 00:29:41.450
parasites upon the parasites.

00:29:41.450 --> 00:29:42.860
They encode nothing.

00:29:42.860 --> 00:29:45.360
All they do is they make an RNA
that doesn't get translated.

00:29:45.360 --> 00:29:46.280
There's no open reading frame.

00:29:46.280 --> 00:29:47.488
They go off to the cytoplasm.

00:29:47.488 --> 00:29:50.060
The only thing this RNA's
supposedly really good at

00:29:50.060 --> 00:29:53.992
is grabbing the LINE
machinery away from a LINE RNA

00:29:53.992 --> 00:29:56.200
and getting it to stick it
in the chromosome instead.

00:29:56.200 --> 00:29:57.020
[LAUGHTER]

00:29:57.020 --> 00:29:58.150
It's just-- it's just--

00:29:58.150 --> 00:30:00.650
they have a lot of millions of
years to work this stuff out.

00:30:00.650 --> 00:30:02.750
And it's very clever.

00:30:02.750 --> 00:30:05.420
Retrovirus-like
elements, these things

00:30:05.420 --> 00:30:08.720
also move by RNA intermediates
by a different mechanism.

00:30:08.720 --> 00:30:11.781
They involve a gag and a
pol but not an env gene.

00:30:11.781 --> 00:30:13.280
But otherwise they
look an awful lot

00:30:13.280 --> 00:30:15.170
like the mechanism
of retroviruses.

00:30:15.170 --> 00:30:17.150
And, indeed, it is
thought that these

00:30:17.150 --> 00:30:18.890
were the precursors
to retroviruses,

00:30:18.890 --> 00:30:21.620
that they learned first how
to do their jumping jacks

00:30:21.620 --> 00:30:22.802
within a cell.

00:30:22.802 --> 00:30:25.010
And then, after having worked
out how to do all this,

00:30:25.010 --> 00:30:26.426
get reverse-transcribed,
whatever,

00:30:26.426 --> 00:30:29.360
pick up a cellular
envelope gene, and depart.

00:30:29.360 --> 00:30:31.270
Then there's DNA transposons.

00:30:31.270 --> 00:30:33.020
They're very different
to the other three,

00:30:33.020 --> 00:30:34.280
because they don't make RNAs.

00:30:34.280 --> 00:30:36.004
What they do is
they make a message

00:30:36.004 --> 00:30:37.670
that goes-- sorry--
they don't transpose

00:30:37.670 --> 00:30:38.820
through RNA intermediates.

00:30:38.820 --> 00:30:41.070
They make a message, it goes
off, makes a transposase,

00:30:41.070 --> 00:30:42.820
the transposase comes
back to the nucleus,

00:30:42.820 --> 00:30:47.264
cuts the chromosome and moves
the DNA element elsewhere.

00:30:47.264 --> 00:30:49.680
These guys have a very different
life than the other guys,

00:30:49.680 --> 00:30:52.500
because this is actually
a pretty crummy lifestyle.

00:30:52.500 --> 00:30:54.390
If you're a transposase,
well, the problem

00:30:54.390 --> 00:30:56.500
is that your transposase
gets made out there

00:30:56.500 --> 00:30:58.590
in the cytoplasm, comes
back to the nucleus,

00:30:58.590 --> 00:31:00.154
and tries to find who made it.

00:31:00.154 --> 00:31:02.070
It would like to find
the element that made it

00:31:02.070 --> 00:31:03.624
and move that element
preferentially.

00:31:03.624 --> 00:31:05.040
Remember, the LINE
is smart enough

00:31:05.040 --> 00:31:07.144
that the proteins grab
the RNA that made it.

00:31:07.144 --> 00:31:09.560
That way, you ensure that
you're moving an active element.

00:31:09.560 --> 00:31:12.018
The bloody transposase comes
back, doesn't know who to get!

00:31:12.018 --> 00:31:13.640
It grabs anybody and moves it.

00:31:13.640 --> 00:31:14.140
[LAUGHTER]

00:31:14.140 --> 00:31:17.680
And therefore DNA transposons
are self-limiting infections.

00:31:17.680 --> 00:31:20.506
Because, as they begin to build
up extra defective copies,

00:31:20.506 --> 00:31:22.380
they become less and
less and less efficient,

00:31:22.380 --> 00:31:23.880
and their lives are short.

00:31:23.880 --> 00:31:24.647
OK?

00:31:24.647 --> 00:31:25.730
So that's what you've got.

00:31:25.730 --> 00:31:29.000
Now, a few comments.

00:31:29.000 --> 00:31:31.280
We can put them all
into a family tree.

00:31:31.280 --> 00:31:32.780
Because every
element, when it hops,

00:31:32.780 --> 00:31:36.860
is a copy of something that was
an active element, initially.

00:31:36.860 --> 00:31:41.319
Its siblings who hopped
about the same time all

00:31:41.319 --> 00:31:43.110
started with the same
sequence, and then it

00:31:43.110 --> 00:31:44.540
began to degenerate.

00:31:44.540 --> 00:31:47.330
But we can, by evolutionary,
by phylogenetic reconstruction,

00:31:47.330 --> 00:31:50.840
figure out that these 52
SINE elements are siblings

00:31:50.840 --> 00:31:54.080
and they arose by virtue of
the number of changes that

00:31:54.080 --> 00:31:55.850
are in them, about,
oh, I don't know,

00:31:55.850 --> 00:31:57.980
5 million years or
10 million years.

00:31:57.980 --> 00:31:59.990
And every single
element in the genome

00:31:59.990 --> 00:32:02.829
can be put into families, and
those families can be dated.

00:32:02.829 --> 00:32:05.120
So, in fact, we've done that
for all 3 million elements

00:32:05.120 --> 00:32:06.080
of the genome.

00:32:06.080 --> 00:32:08.570
And those dates are
really fascinating.

00:32:08.570 --> 00:32:11.060
In particular, you can find
out that most of our repeats

00:32:11.060 --> 00:32:13.010
are very, very,
very old, much older

00:32:13.010 --> 00:32:15.560
than you see in flies or
worms or mustard weeds.

00:32:15.560 --> 00:32:17.380
This tells, by
measure, by percentage

00:32:17.380 --> 00:32:18.680
of interspersed repeats.

00:32:18.680 --> 00:32:21.110
Another way to put it, though,
is for the human genome

00:32:21.110 --> 00:32:24.170
the vast majority of repeats
in your genome hopped

00:32:24.170 --> 00:32:28.110
before mammals, hopped before
the mammalian radiation.

00:32:28.110 --> 00:32:31.290
There are fossils pre mammal.

00:32:31.290 --> 00:32:33.500
And so they're quite old things.

00:32:33.500 --> 00:32:34.721
But here's a shocker.

00:32:34.721 --> 00:32:36.470
Despite the fact that
they've been hopping

00:32:36.470 --> 00:32:39.680
for a billion years, despite
the fact that this is, you know,

00:32:39.680 --> 00:32:42.450
the thing that's caused
your genome to form,

00:32:42.450 --> 00:32:47.210
at the size it is, the
rate of transposition

00:32:47.210 --> 00:32:49.970
is plummeting dramatically.

00:32:49.970 --> 00:32:52.710
In fact, in olden days--

00:32:52.710 --> 00:32:53.510
is this working?

00:32:53.510 --> 00:32:54.010
Yeah.

00:32:54.010 --> 00:32:55.870
In old days, here--

00:32:55.870 --> 00:32:57.514
this is old, moving to now--

00:32:57.514 --> 00:32:58.930
the rate of
transmission was here.

00:32:58.930 --> 00:33:00.030
It went up.

00:33:00.030 --> 00:33:02.650
But lately, the last
40 million years, it

00:33:02.650 --> 00:33:05.990
has been in a plummet.

00:33:05.990 --> 00:33:11.180
In fact, DNA transposons are
extinct, completely extinct.

00:33:11.180 --> 00:33:17.040
These retrovirus-like guys are
almost extinct, if not extinct.

00:33:17.040 --> 00:33:19.160
There is only a
single family left

00:33:19.160 --> 00:33:22.400
that has hopped since our
divergence from chimpanzees.

00:33:22.400 --> 00:33:26.390
And we can only detect three
possible active elements

00:33:26.390 --> 00:33:28.820
that have open reading
frames that are full-length.

00:33:28.820 --> 00:33:30.920
If those guys have
[? missensed ?] mutations that

00:33:30.920 --> 00:33:33.200
inactivate them, that's it.

00:33:33.200 --> 00:33:33.910
They're dead.

00:33:33.910 --> 00:33:36.840
That's the end of the line
for LTR retrotransposition.

00:33:36.840 --> 00:33:39.110
So there is a serious
ecological crisis,

00:33:39.110 --> 00:33:41.930
with regard to the, you
know, endangered species

00:33:41.930 --> 00:33:43.670
of transposons in
our genome right now.

00:33:43.670 --> 00:33:44.210
[LAUGHTER]

00:33:44.210 --> 00:33:45.440
And we don't know why.

00:33:45.440 --> 00:33:48.500
But we do know that this is
not happening in the mouse.

00:33:48.500 --> 00:33:50.360
Interestingly,
the mouse does not

00:33:50.360 --> 00:33:52.220
show the same pattern at all.

00:33:52.220 --> 00:33:55.490
Curiously, its rate of
transposition is not--

00:33:55.490 --> 00:33:58.550
and there are 11 happy families
of LTR retrotransposons,

00:33:58.550 --> 00:34:01.100
hopping around the
mouse genome, every day.

00:34:01.100 --> 00:34:02.137
What's gone on?

00:34:02.137 --> 00:34:02.720
We don't know.

00:34:02.720 --> 00:34:04.261
We have some
interesting speculations

00:34:04.261 --> 00:34:08.570
having to do with the fact
that it may be that our genome,

00:34:08.570 --> 00:34:11.300
that our population, the
[? prominent ?] lineage,

00:34:11.300 --> 00:34:13.130
has a very small
population size,

00:34:13.130 --> 00:34:15.860
and we're much more subject to
the effects of genetic drift

00:34:15.860 --> 00:34:17.750
that could let you
lose the small number

00:34:17.750 --> 00:34:19.050
of active elements.

00:34:19.050 --> 00:34:21.383
So that's my own guess, but
we have no evidence of this.

00:34:21.383 --> 00:34:24.739
Rodents tend to have much larger
population sizes are much less

00:34:24.739 --> 00:34:26.000
subject to genetic drift.

00:34:26.000 --> 00:34:27.750
But we're going to
have to look at a bunch

00:34:27.750 --> 00:34:29.550
more species to
know what's gone on.

00:34:29.550 --> 00:34:31.520
Here's another weird
fact, just to show you

00:34:31.520 --> 00:34:33.270
what you can learn by
staring at a genome.

00:34:33.270 --> 00:34:35.639
You think genome
sequences are boring?

00:34:35.639 --> 00:34:36.230
Wow.

00:34:36.230 --> 00:34:38.929
Here's something really weird.

00:34:38.929 --> 00:34:41.420
If you were a transposon,
where would you go?

00:34:41.420 --> 00:34:45.560
Would you hop into
gene-rich, GC-rich DNA?

00:34:45.560 --> 00:34:50.690
Or would you hop into
gene-poor, AT-rich DNA?

00:34:50.690 --> 00:34:52.400
Well, if you were
a smart transposon,

00:34:52.400 --> 00:34:54.650
trying to get along
with your organism,

00:34:54.650 --> 00:34:57.620
it'd be sensible to hop into
gene-poor, AT-rich DNA, where

00:34:57.620 --> 00:34:59.150
you'd do the least damage.

00:34:59.150 --> 00:35:01.730
And, in fact, that's exactly
what the LINE element does.

00:35:01.730 --> 00:35:05.960
LINE's endonuclease cuts
the chromosome at AAAAT.

00:35:05.960 --> 00:35:08.450
And, in fact, that accounts
for the tremendous enrichment

00:35:08.450 --> 00:35:11.745
of LINE elements in
AT-rich, gene-poor regions.

00:35:11.745 --> 00:35:14.760
Seems a very sensible sort of
detente between the organism

00:35:14.760 --> 00:35:17.930
and the genes, the organism
and the transposons.

00:35:17.930 --> 00:35:22.150
SINE elements, however, show
exactly the opposite pattern.

00:35:22.150 --> 00:35:24.790
They are highly
enriched near genes.

00:35:24.790 --> 00:35:27.700
Now, A, that doesn't
make a lot of sense.

00:35:27.700 --> 00:35:28.950
Well, why should they do that?

00:35:28.950 --> 00:35:31.074
And B, far more importantly,
from the point of view

00:35:31.074 --> 00:35:33.380
of biochemical mechanism,
how do they accomplish that?

00:35:33.380 --> 00:35:34.755
Because I thought
I told you they

00:35:34.755 --> 00:35:36.870
use the LINE
endonuclease to move.

00:35:36.870 --> 00:35:39.830
Why don't they get
stuck in at AAAAT?

00:35:39.830 --> 00:35:40.690
Two hypotheses.

00:35:40.690 --> 00:35:44.560
Number 1, somehow
that RNA is so smart

00:35:44.560 --> 00:35:46.630
that it could reprogram
the endonuclease

00:35:46.630 --> 00:35:47.964
to put it elsewhere.

00:35:47.964 --> 00:35:49.130
I don't know how to do that.

00:35:49.130 --> 00:35:51.796
That's be a fancy bit of protein
machinery, protein engineering,

00:35:51.796 --> 00:35:52.560
but maybe.

00:35:52.560 --> 00:35:55.080
Alternative hypothesis--
they actually

00:35:55.080 --> 00:35:58.320
go in in AT-rich
regions, but evolution

00:35:58.320 --> 00:36:00.180
favors the retention
of those that are

00:36:00.180 --> 00:36:02.872
near genes in GC-rich regions.

00:36:02.872 --> 00:36:04.830
But the latter hypothesis
that I've just stated

00:36:04.830 --> 00:36:06.800
would imply a function.

00:36:06.800 --> 00:36:08.520
Well, we now know the
answer, because we

00:36:08.520 --> 00:36:11.320
can look at the genome and the
genome will tell us the answer.

00:36:11.320 --> 00:36:12.240
How do we know?

00:36:12.240 --> 00:36:14.370
We can take all those
SINE transposons

00:36:14.370 --> 00:36:17.460
and sort them into the newborn
ones, the adolescent ones,

00:36:17.460 --> 00:36:20.750
the middle-aged ones,
and the old ones.

00:36:20.750 --> 00:36:23.450
The newborn ones have a
distribution that look just

00:36:23.450 --> 00:36:25.850
like the LINEs in AT-rich DNA.

00:36:25.850 --> 00:36:28.160
As they get older
and older and older,

00:36:28.160 --> 00:36:32.220
there's a 13-fold enrichment
for those near genes.

00:36:32.220 --> 00:36:35.240
While there are some alternative
possible interpretations

00:36:35.240 --> 00:36:37.540
for that, we strongly
favor the interpretation

00:36:37.540 --> 00:36:40.177
that there's actually
selection going on for this.

00:36:40.177 --> 00:36:42.260
And there was a hypothesis
proposed two years ago,

00:36:42.260 --> 00:36:44.870
by [? Schmid, ?] that's
really fascinating that

00:36:44.870 --> 00:36:46.760
proposes the mechanism.

00:36:46.760 --> 00:36:48.620
Turns out that
these SINE RNAs bind

00:36:48.620 --> 00:36:51.380
in a sequence-specific fashion
to a specific protein kinase

00:36:51.380 --> 00:36:53.690
involved in the regulation
of protein translation.

00:36:53.690 --> 00:36:55.940
Different organisms
have different SINEs,

00:36:55.940 --> 00:36:58.820
but they all blind to the
cognate protein kinase.

00:36:58.820 --> 00:37:01.240
Mutate them
slightly, they don't.

00:37:01.240 --> 00:37:03.250
If you wanted to regulate
protein translation,

00:37:03.250 --> 00:37:04.850
how would you do it?

00:37:04.850 --> 00:37:07.310
Would you use a protein--
oh, sorry, one more fact.

00:37:07.310 --> 00:37:09.980
SINEs, unlike other
transposable elements,

00:37:09.980 --> 00:37:14.300
are expressed in somatic cells
under stress; others, just

00:37:14.300 --> 00:37:15.870
in the germline.

00:37:15.870 --> 00:37:18.420
Turns out, if you wanted to
regulate protein translation

00:37:18.420 --> 00:37:21.984
under stress, would it be
smart to use a protein?

00:37:21.984 --> 00:37:23.650
Nah, because you'd
have to translate it.

00:37:23.650 --> 00:37:23.860
Right?

00:37:23.860 --> 00:37:25.060
And that'd be kind of dumb.

00:37:25.060 --> 00:37:26.062
You'd use an RNA.

00:37:26.062 --> 00:37:27.520
And you'd want to
have a lot of it,

00:37:27.520 --> 00:37:29.412
if you wanted to work
quickly under stress.

00:37:29.412 --> 00:37:31.120
And you'd want it to
be located somewhere

00:37:31.120 --> 00:37:33.539
in open chromatin, where
you could express it easily.

00:37:33.539 --> 00:37:35.830
In other words-- of course,
this is backward reasoning,

00:37:35.830 --> 00:37:40.450
but it might be quite reasonable
to use a high-copy-repeat RNA,

00:37:40.450 --> 00:37:43.120
located near genes, if you
wanted to regulate something

00:37:43.120 --> 00:37:44.290
as a stress response.

00:37:44.290 --> 00:37:46.290
In any case, this was
[? Schmid's ?] hypothesis.

00:37:46.290 --> 00:37:47.770
He had made it two years ago.

00:37:47.770 --> 00:37:49.900
And the evidence we
have now, that there

00:37:49.900 --> 00:37:52.270
is a rapid, within
25 million years,

00:37:52.270 --> 00:37:54.880
reshaping of the
distribution of SINEs I think

00:37:54.880 --> 00:37:58.540
provides a very interesting bit
of potentially corroborating

00:37:58.540 --> 00:37:59.830
evidence for that.

00:37:59.830 --> 00:38:02.320
Here's another interesting fact.

00:38:02.320 --> 00:38:06.430
You can take transposons that
were born at the same time

00:38:06.430 --> 00:38:08.350
and use them for epidemiology.

00:38:08.350 --> 00:38:10.360
You can use them as if
they were a birth cohort

00:38:10.360 --> 00:38:12.790
and say, if a transposon
landed on the Y

00:38:12.790 --> 00:38:16.020
chromosome versus the X
chromosome, how does it do?

00:38:16.020 --> 00:38:16.695
Is that right?

00:38:16.695 --> 00:38:18.610
Yeah, that's right.

00:38:18.610 --> 00:38:20.240
How does it do?

00:38:20.240 --> 00:38:22.760
Well, if you look
at it, transposons

00:38:22.760 --> 00:38:25.070
born at the same time
that land on the Y

00:38:25.070 --> 00:38:28.400
have lots more mutations
than land on the X.

00:38:28.400 --> 00:38:30.140
And from this you can infer--

00:38:30.140 --> 00:38:31.910
and, in fact, back out--

00:38:31.910 --> 00:38:34.520
that the rate of
mutation in sperm--

00:38:34.520 --> 00:38:38.120
because Y chromosomes always
go through male meiosis--

00:38:38.120 --> 00:38:40.520
is twice as high as in eggs.

00:38:40.520 --> 00:38:42.820
Because X chromosomes spent
two thirds of their time

00:38:42.820 --> 00:38:43.929
in female meiosis.

00:38:43.929 --> 00:38:46.220
So you can, in fact, work
out the differential mutation

00:38:46.220 --> 00:38:49.700
rates, by comparing things on
the X and the Y chromosome.

00:38:49.700 --> 00:38:52.040
In fact, in this
particular case,

00:38:52.040 --> 00:38:54.290
we have the pleasure of
confirming work of David Page,

00:38:54.290 --> 00:38:55.409
about six months ago.

00:38:55.409 --> 00:38:57.200
There had been a bunch
of previous studies,

00:38:57.200 --> 00:38:59.283
trying to do this, that
got lots of wrong answers.

00:38:59.283 --> 00:39:01.340
And David made a really
nice estimate of this,

00:39:01.340 --> 00:39:02.879
about six months
ago, where he said,

00:39:02.879 --> 00:39:04.670
we really think it is
a twofold, difference

00:39:04.670 --> 00:39:07.740
based on one particular region,
about four million years ago.

00:39:07.740 --> 00:39:09.170
And we can confirm
this now, based

00:39:09.170 --> 00:39:11.360
on about 100 times as
many data, spread out

00:39:11.360 --> 00:39:15.500
over 40 million years,
that David's suggested rate

00:39:15.500 --> 00:39:20.347
of male-to-female
mutation is spot-on.

00:39:20.347 --> 00:39:22.430
There are zillions of more
things I could tell you

00:39:22.430 --> 00:39:23.330
about transposons.

00:39:23.330 --> 00:39:24.000
I won't.

00:39:24.000 --> 00:39:25.580
You know, I'd tell
you about the fact

00:39:25.580 --> 00:39:27.422
that we get genes
from transposons.

00:39:27.422 --> 00:39:28.880
At least 50 genes
in the genome, we

00:39:28.880 --> 00:39:31.370
can recognize as gifts
from transposons.

00:39:31.370 --> 00:39:33.320
We can also recognize
the most recent ones

00:39:33.320 --> 00:39:36.460
to hop, the ones that are still
variable in our population

00:39:36.460 --> 00:39:39.930
and could be used to trace
the history of our population.

00:39:39.930 --> 00:39:42.650
But let me switch to genes,
because our genome does

00:39:42.650 --> 00:39:45.176
contain genes, and they
are of some interest.

00:39:45.176 --> 00:39:46.850
[LAUGHTER]

00:39:46.850 --> 00:39:50.150
We have been attempting,
by looking at the sequence,

00:39:50.150 --> 00:39:53.300
to identify the gene
content of the human genome.

00:39:53.300 --> 00:39:54.237
Golly.

00:39:54.237 --> 00:39:56.570
That turns out to be a lot
harder than bacteria or yeast

00:39:56.570 --> 00:39:58.817
or worms or flies,
because there's

00:39:58.817 --> 00:39:59.900
a signal-to-noise problem.

00:39:59.900 --> 00:40:01.970
Our genes are only 1
and 1/2% of our genome,

00:40:01.970 --> 00:40:03.830
whereas in flies
and worms and yeast

00:40:03.830 --> 00:40:07.460
they're a big proportion
of a [? genetic ?] locus

00:40:07.460 --> 00:40:08.950
is the coding regions.

00:40:08.950 --> 00:40:10.890
50%, typically.

00:40:10.890 --> 00:40:12.860
Whereas in us it's
a trivial fraction.

00:40:12.860 --> 00:40:15.780
And so our ability to detect
these is nowhere near as good,

00:40:15.780 --> 00:40:18.260
and the gene set currently
generated and described

00:40:18.260 --> 00:40:20.810
in the paper is
nowhere near as good.

00:40:20.810 --> 00:40:22.926
But, nonetheless, we
have a pretty good handle

00:40:22.926 --> 00:40:24.800
on how many genes there
are, and we certainly

00:40:24.800 --> 00:40:27.960
do have good chunks
of most of them.

00:40:27.960 --> 00:40:30.410
And the answer is, there's
really only about 30,000,

00:40:30.410 --> 00:40:31.520
35,000 genes.

00:40:31.520 --> 00:40:34.340
I'll say 35,000 genes,
as a rough guess.

00:40:34.340 --> 00:40:37.100
We've run it through all sorts
of estimation techniques,

00:40:37.100 --> 00:40:38.872
statistical
estimation techniques.

00:40:38.872 --> 00:40:40.580
We've also done direct
counting programs,

00:40:40.580 --> 00:40:43.130
including one written
by Chris Burge here

00:40:43.130 --> 00:40:47.510
called Genome Scam, which is a
successor to his famous GENSCAN

00:40:47.510 --> 00:40:50.722
program, for finding
genes within genomes,

00:40:50.722 --> 00:40:51.930
as well as a bunch of others.

00:40:51.930 --> 00:40:53.690
And all the evidence
we get points

00:40:53.690 --> 00:40:57.800
to the same conclusion--
about 35,000 genes.

00:40:57.800 --> 00:41:01.190
So I owe an apology, and I wish
to make that apology right here

00:41:01.190 --> 00:41:05.030
and now, to about seven years'
worth of students in my 701

00:41:05.030 --> 00:41:08.780
course, because I have been
reporting the official number,

00:41:08.780 --> 00:41:11.820
that there are supposed to be
100,000 genes in the genome.

00:41:11.820 --> 00:41:14.540
And so I don't know that you're
entitled to your money back,

00:41:14.540 --> 00:41:15.290
but, nonetheless--

00:41:15.290 --> 00:41:16.550
[LAUGHTER]

00:41:16.550 --> 00:41:18.120
Check with the
registrar on that.

00:41:18.120 --> 00:41:24.046
But, nonetheless, I now wish
to correct that misimpression.

00:41:24.046 --> 00:41:26.420
Actually, why did we ever
think there were 100,000 genes?

00:41:26.420 --> 00:41:28.130
That turns out to
be very interesting.

00:41:28.130 --> 00:41:30.950
We had to track back to what
was the origin of this round,

00:41:30.950 --> 00:41:32.420
nice 100,000-gene number.

00:41:32.420 --> 00:41:35.080
The origin turns out, very
clearly, to be down the road.

00:41:35.080 --> 00:41:36.420
It's Wally Gilbert.

00:41:36.420 --> 00:41:38.870
Wally did a beautifully
simple calculation,

00:41:38.870 --> 00:41:41.300
in the early 1980s, that
a typical human gene

00:41:41.300 --> 00:41:43.400
looked like it was
about 30,000 letters.

00:41:43.400 --> 00:41:45.440
The genome was about
3 billion letters.

00:41:45.440 --> 00:41:47.900
Divide one into the
other, you get 100,000.

00:41:47.900 --> 00:41:50.420
And it's so round, it
made it into the textbooks

00:41:50.420 --> 00:41:51.825
and just refused to go.

00:41:51.825 --> 00:41:53.450
And, of course, it
was only meant to be

00:41:53.450 --> 00:41:54.680
a back-of-the-envelope estimate.

00:41:54.680 --> 00:41:56.690
If you tell this to Wally,
the reaction is, hey,

00:41:56.690 --> 00:41:58.106
you know, we were
basically right.

00:41:58.106 --> 00:42:01.479
Because-- Wally's trained as a
physicist, and, to a physicist,

00:42:01.479 --> 00:42:04.020
a back-of-the-envelope estimate
that's within a half an order

00:42:04.020 --> 00:42:05.384
of magnitude is just fine.

00:42:05.384 --> 00:42:06.050
The problem is--

00:42:06.050 --> 00:42:07.190
[LAUGHTER]

00:42:07.190 --> 00:42:09.860
--that biologists think
that a factor of three

00:42:09.860 --> 00:42:10.810
means something.

00:42:10.810 --> 00:42:11.663
Right?

00:42:11.663 --> 00:42:12.630
[LAUGHTER]

00:42:12.630 --> 00:42:15.120
And it really doesn't, not by
these estimation techniques,

00:42:15.120 --> 00:42:15.620
anyway.

00:42:15.620 --> 00:42:17.180
But there are a lot fewer genes.

00:42:17.180 --> 00:42:18.110
We've taken the genes.

00:42:18.110 --> 00:42:20.060
We've sorted them into
functional categories,

00:42:20.060 --> 00:42:21.260
and things like that.

00:42:21.260 --> 00:42:22.970
We can say a number
of things about them.

00:42:22.970 --> 00:42:25.730
The exons tend to be
smaller, the introns--

00:42:25.730 --> 00:42:27.470
the intervening spaces-- bigger.

00:42:27.470 --> 00:42:29.340
There seems to be
more alternative

00:42:29.340 --> 00:42:31.340
splicing, about twofold
more alternative

00:42:31.340 --> 00:42:35.840
splicing, in human genes, it
appears, than flies or worms.

00:42:35.840 --> 00:42:38.897
So that, in fact,
although we don't--

00:42:38.897 --> 00:42:41.480
The depressing fact is that the
total number of genes we have,

00:42:41.480 --> 00:42:45.260
at 35,000, is only
about twice as many

00:42:45.260 --> 00:42:48.890
as a nematode worm, C. elegans,
or only about twice as many

00:42:48.890 --> 00:42:51.260
as the fruit fly
Drosophila melanogaster.

00:42:51.260 --> 00:42:52.730
And, on the whole,
we like to think

00:42:52.730 --> 00:42:54.740
that we're a lot more
complicated than that.

00:42:54.740 --> 00:42:57.359
And so this has provoked
tremendous concern,

00:42:57.359 --> 00:42:59.150
particularly in the
past week, about people

00:42:59.150 --> 00:43:01.430
worrying about how we can
be so complex with such

00:43:01.430 --> 00:43:02.829
an economy of genes.

00:43:02.829 --> 00:43:05.120
One answer is, well, we make
more proteins out of them.

00:43:05.120 --> 00:43:07.220
We do more alternative
splicing that clearly does

00:43:07.220 --> 00:43:09.290
seem to be right.

00:43:09.290 --> 00:43:11.600
Oh, another fact to mention
is that they are, indeed--

00:43:11.600 --> 00:43:13.970
because now we can
tell-- a huge, huge bias

00:43:13.970 --> 00:43:16.890
to the GC-rich regions, in case
you needed any confirmation

00:43:16.890 --> 00:43:17.390
about this.

00:43:17.390 --> 00:43:19.816
About a tenfold
enrichment, there.

00:43:19.816 --> 00:43:22.190
But, when we look at our genes,
we ask, how are they new?

00:43:22.190 --> 00:43:23.180
How are they different?

00:43:23.180 --> 00:43:26.300
What explains what
makes vertebrate genes?

00:43:26.300 --> 00:43:30.110
Well, first off, we are
not that innovative.

00:43:30.110 --> 00:43:34.265
Most of the domains in
our proteins, they're old.

00:43:34.265 --> 00:43:35.870
They're in invertebrates.

00:43:35.870 --> 00:43:38.330
Only about 7% of all
the domains that we

00:43:38.330 --> 00:43:40.220
can find in vertebrate
proteins appear

00:43:40.220 --> 00:43:43.310
to be new to the
vertebrate lineages.

00:43:43.310 --> 00:43:46.290
That is a surprise.

00:43:46.290 --> 00:43:48.364
There's only one
enzyme that vertebrates

00:43:48.364 --> 00:43:49.280
seem to have invented.

00:43:49.280 --> 00:43:52.560
That's pancreatic ribonuclease.

00:43:52.560 --> 00:43:55.700
But what we did do,
to get more genes,

00:43:55.700 --> 00:43:57.410
was we created many
more architectures.

00:43:57.410 --> 00:44:00.440
We put together the old
pieces in many new ways.

00:44:00.440 --> 00:44:02.750
And we have considerably
more architectures,

00:44:02.750 --> 00:44:07.070
any given domain appears with
many more genes in a human

00:44:07.070 --> 00:44:09.590
than it does typically
in fly or worm.

00:44:09.590 --> 00:44:12.050
There's a particular
emphasis of this

00:44:12.050 --> 00:44:13.590
in extracellular molecules.

00:44:13.590 --> 00:44:15.924
And I expect this has something
to do with the greater

00:44:15.924 --> 00:44:17.840
complexity of our circuits,
is that we connect

00:44:17.840 --> 00:44:21.170
the parts in many
different ways.

00:44:21.170 --> 00:44:25.640
In addition, another bit of our
rather derivative innovation

00:44:25.640 --> 00:44:29.270
is that, when we like something,
we just expand it dramatically.

00:44:29.270 --> 00:44:31.100
Olfactory receptors.

00:44:31.100 --> 00:44:35.810
The human genome shows about
1,000 olfactory-receptor loci--

00:44:35.810 --> 00:44:36.800
1,000.

00:44:36.800 --> 00:44:38.840
We actually count
906, at the moment,

00:44:38.840 --> 00:44:40.007
that we've got well in hand.

00:44:40.007 --> 00:44:41.964
And then there are parts
missing, and all that,

00:44:41.964 --> 00:44:42.910
but it's about right.

00:44:42.910 --> 00:44:46.470
Shows that vertebrates show
tremendous interest in smell.

00:44:46.470 --> 00:44:48.960
It's one of the crowning
achievements of vertebrates,

00:44:48.960 --> 00:44:52.570
is the spectacular elaboration
of smell receptors.

00:44:52.570 --> 00:44:56.030
However, I'm sorry to report
that, having done this,

00:44:56.030 --> 00:44:58.200
the hominid lineage decided
that it didn't really

00:44:58.200 --> 00:45:00.030
care about smell receptors.

00:45:00.030 --> 00:45:02.100
And two thirds of
our smell receptors

00:45:02.100 --> 00:45:04.740
are pseudogenes, we can
clearly see from the sequence,

00:45:04.740 --> 00:45:07.740
whereas mouse keeps its smell
receptors in pretty fine

00:45:07.740 --> 00:45:09.550
working order, by comparison.

00:45:09.550 --> 00:45:11.760
We seem, after all this
investment in the nose,

00:45:11.760 --> 00:45:13.380
to have decided to
settle on the eyes,

00:45:13.380 --> 00:45:15.540
instead, as a more
interesting sense.

00:45:15.540 --> 00:45:18.720
And you can see it in what
we tidy up in our genome.

00:45:18.720 --> 00:45:21.000
Immunoglobulin domains,
obviously a big part

00:45:21.000 --> 00:45:21.910
of the genome.

00:45:21.910 --> 00:45:23.250
We discuss some of that.

00:45:23.250 --> 00:45:24.675
Intermediate filaments.

00:45:24.675 --> 00:45:26.920
There are 127 intermediate
filament proteins,

00:45:26.920 --> 00:45:28.749
including 111 keratins.

00:45:28.749 --> 00:45:30.290
If you want to
describe a vertebrate,

00:45:30.290 --> 00:45:31.790
you'd say it's
something that really

00:45:31.790 --> 00:45:36.090
likes epithelial surfaces, all
sorts of specialized epithelia,

00:45:36.090 --> 00:45:37.590
for these intermediate
filaments.

00:45:37.590 --> 00:45:38.325
Growth factors.

00:45:38.325 --> 00:45:40.080
We're into growth factors.

00:45:40.080 --> 00:45:42.210
Big expansions [INAUDIBLE]
growth-factor families,

00:45:42.210 --> 00:45:43.530
like TGF beta.

00:45:43.530 --> 00:45:47.680
So, vertebrates are
things that like to smell,

00:45:47.680 --> 00:45:50.550
have immune systems,
specialized epithelia,

00:45:50.550 --> 00:45:52.920
and communicate and develop
with lots of growth factors.

00:45:52.920 --> 00:45:54.930
That [INAUDIBLE] be us.

00:45:54.930 --> 00:45:57.660
Also, we appear not to be
able to take credit for that

00:45:57.660 --> 00:45:59.872
much-- for all of our genes.

00:45:59.872 --> 00:46:02.730
Actually, about 223,
is our count, genes,

00:46:02.730 --> 00:46:05.190
that we find in the human,
there is no homologue,

00:46:05.190 --> 00:46:10.670
outside of vertebrates, in the
rest of the eukaryotic world,

00:46:10.670 --> 00:46:12.980
but there are
homologues in bacteria.

00:46:12.980 --> 00:46:16.070
That is, we have a close
relative in bacteria

00:46:16.070 --> 00:46:19.410
but nothing outside
of vertebrata.

00:46:19.410 --> 00:46:21.330
The likely
interpretation of this

00:46:21.330 --> 00:46:23.670
is that these represent
horizontal gene transfers that

00:46:23.670 --> 00:46:26.190
occurred early in the
vertebrate lineage.

00:46:26.190 --> 00:46:29.430
Bacteria can, in fact, mate
with nonbacterial cells

00:46:29.430 --> 00:46:31.017
and transfer DNA to them.

00:46:31.017 --> 00:46:32.100
It's usually unproductive.

00:46:32.100 --> 00:46:35.010
But, in fact, possibly
back when we were a fish,

00:46:35.010 --> 00:46:38.209
some bacteria got with some
gamete and passed some DNA.

00:46:38.209 --> 00:46:39.750
And this isn't a
frequent occurrence,

00:46:39.750 --> 00:46:41.550
but we count about 223 of these.

00:46:41.550 --> 00:46:43.969
It's, of course, conceivable
that some of these

00:46:43.969 --> 00:46:45.510
are hiding somewhere
in invertebrates

00:46:45.510 --> 00:46:46.550
we haven't seen yet.

00:46:46.550 --> 00:46:47.799
And we may have to rethink it.

00:46:47.799 --> 00:46:49.740
But, right now, the most
likely interpretation

00:46:49.740 --> 00:46:51.754
is that these are indeed
horizontal transfers.

00:46:51.754 --> 00:46:53.670
They're all enzymes, so
we didn't invent them.

00:46:53.670 --> 00:46:54.690
They're not
characteristic of what

00:46:54.690 --> 00:46:56.010
gets invented in vertebrates.

00:46:56.010 --> 00:46:59.350
One of them, for example,
is monoamine oxidase,

00:46:59.350 --> 00:47:01.510
important in
psychiatric disease.

00:47:01.510 --> 00:47:04.711
Now, to extract all of the
information out of the genome,

00:47:04.711 --> 00:47:06.960
we're going to need to line
up with lots and lots more

00:47:06.960 --> 00:47:07.440
species.

00:47:07.440 --> 00:47:09.231
And so already the
international consortium

00:47:09.231 --> 00:47:14.040
is at work on sequencing mouse,
rat, zebrafish, pufferfish,

00:47:14.040 --> 00:47:16.360
and various other species.

00:47:16.360 --> 00:47:18.810
In a partnership
between Whitehead, Wash.

00:47:18.810 --> 00:47:21.450
U, and Sanger, we've been
generating random coverage

00:47:21.450 --> 00:47:23.670
of the mouse genome, to
be able to pick out exons

00:47:23.670 --> 00:47:25.020
by cross-species homology.

00:47:25.020 --> 00:47:27.034
The goal is to get about
threefold coverage.

00:47:27.034 --> 00:47:28.450
We're already at
twofold coverage,

00:47:28.450 --> 00:47:30.270
so I think the goal of
threefold coverage by the spring

00:47:30.270 --> 00:47:32.100
will be met, and we'll
be able to line up

00:47:32.100 --> 00:47:35.190
and, I think, identify
many of the exons

00:47:35.190 --> 00:47:37.140
from the mouse rather directly.

00:47:37.140 --> 00:47:38.848
But I think we're
going to be able to get

00:47:38.848 --> 00:47:40.230
a lot more than just the exons.

00:47:40.230 --> 00:47:41.646
Because, in fact,
I think we'll be

00:47:41.646 --> 00:47:43.872
able to get much of the
regulation this way.

00:47:43.872 --> 00:47:45.330
I'll give you just
one neat example

00:47:45.330 --> 00:47:47.790
that emerges from looking
at the genome sequence.

00:47:47.790 --> 00:47:50.430
We have a very important set of
genes involved in development,

00:47:50.430 --> 00:47:52.530
called the HOX clusters.

00:47:52.530 --> 00:47:57.540
These HOX genes occur
in four big clusters--

00:47:57.540 --> 00:47:59.970
HOX A, B, C, and D--

00:47:59.970 --> 00:48:03.930
that all are homologues to
things that arose in insects.

00:48:03.930 --> 00:48:06.060
We've sequenced them now,
not just in the human,

00:48:06.060 --> 00:48:07.435
but we've done
them to completion

00:48:07.435 --> 00:48:09.810
in the mouse, the
rat, and the baboon.

00:48:09.810 --> 00:48:13.450
When you line them up-- and
this is work of [? Ken Doer ?]

00:48:13.450 --> 00:48:14.670
at the genome center--

00:48:14.670 --> 00:48:16.380
they line up spectacularly.

00:48:16.380 --> 00:48:18.880
I don't just mean "good,"
which is what you'd expect.

00:48:18.880 --> 00:48:21.300
But, spectacularly.

00:48:21.300 --> 00:48:24.270
That is, over 100 million
years of evolution,

00:48:24.270 --> 00:48:26.640
they fall perfectly
on a diagonal.

00:48:26.640 --> 00:48:28.710
There's been no
insertion or deletion,

00:48:28.710 --> 00:48:31.320
to speak of, across these loci.

00:48:31.320 --> 00:48:34.420
And, when you look closely
why, when you look closely,

00:48:34.420 --> 00:48:35.530
you see why.

00:48:35.530 --> 00:48:37.470
Remember, I told you
half of your genome,

00:48:37.470 --> 00:48:39.840
50% of your
genome's, repetitive?

00:48:39.840 --> 00:48:44.300
At the HOX cluster, it's 1%.

00:48:44.300 --> 00:48:46.770
The HOX cluster has
essentially no repeats.

00:48:46.770 --> 00:48:49.530
It admits no mutation
to repeat, either

00:48:49.530 --> 00:48:52.120
in the human or the
rodent lineages.

00:48:52.120 --> 00:48:54.320
There's no target for changing.

00:48:54.320 --> 00:48:56.750
That means that
spacing and content

00:48:56.750 --> 00:48:58.370
are very tightly conserved.

00:48:58.370 --> 00:48:59.870
And that suggests
that this thing is

00:48:59.870 --> 00:49:01.647
chockablock full of regulation.

00:49:01.647 --> 00:49:03.980
And, sure enough, when we
line up the different species,

00:49:03.980 --> 00:49:07.280
we can pick out more than
100 apparently conserved

00:49:07.280 --> 00:49:09.050
regulatory features there.

00:49:09.050 --> 00:49:11.802
I don't think this is
particularly special to HOX.

00:49:11.802 --> 00:49:13.760
I think HOX is very
special in the way it's all

00:49:13.760 --> 00:49:15.840
tightly conserved, and
spacing, and all that.

00:49:15.840 --> 00:49:17.740
But I think, in fact, if we
can get the correct alignments

00:49:17.740 --> 00:49:19.198
throughout the rest
of the genomes,

00:49:19.198 --> 00:49:22.370
we will be able to pick up lots
and lots of regulation that

00:49:22.370 --> 00:49:23.960
occurs.

00:49:23.960 --> 00:49:26.030
Finally, I'll
mention very briefly

00:49:26.030 --> 00:49:28.460
that there are a lot of other
applications of this that

00:49:28.460 --> 00:49:32.120
are happening and that also are
represented in the papers that

00:49:32.120 --> 00:49:34.410
appeared yesterday.

00:49:34.410 --> 00:49:38.420
To apply the sequence
to humans in medicine,

00:49:38.420 --> 00:49:41.810
we really need to understand
not just all human sequence

00:49:41.810 --> 00:49:43.634
but all its variation.

00:49:43.634 --> 00:49:45.800
We need to understand what's
the nature of variation

00:49:45.800 --> 00:49:48.341
in the human species, and what's
the nature of those variants

00:49:48.341 --> 00:49:50.030
that cause disease.

00:49:50.030 --> 00:49:51.930
Well, in fact, over the
past couple of years,

00:49:51.930 --> 00:49:52.910
particularly at
the genome center,

00:49:52.910 --> 00:49:54.140
we've been characterizing that.

00:49:54.140 --> 00:49:56.300
We've had a project to not
just sequence the genome

00:49:56.300 --> 00:49:58.100
but begin to collect variants.

00:49:58.100 --> 00:50:00.890
We did a project,
maybe three years ago,

00:50:00.890 --> 00:50:03.080
which at that time
we called, foolishly,

00:50:03.080 --> 00:50:06.170
"Large-scale survey of
variance in the human genome,"

00:50:06.170 --> 00:50:09.320
where we reported about 4,000
single nucleotide differences

00:50:09.320 --> 00:50:11.540
and could report that
the frequency was about 1

00:50:11.540 --> 00:50:13.580
in 1,000-- actually,
[? about ?] 1,300--

00:50:13.580 --> 00:50:15.890
that typical genes had only
two or three or four common

00:50:15.890 --> 00:50:18.680
variants in the population, and
that, in general, we looked--

00:50:18.680 --> 00:50:20.450
despite being 6 million people--

00:50:20.450 --> 00:50:23.850
like we were really a
population of about 10,000.

00:50:23.850 --> 00:50:26.390
And that's right, because we
are a population of 10,000.

00:50:26.390 --> 00:50:29.390
We all trace back to a small
found population in Africa,

00:50:29.390 --> 00:50:32.120
a mere 5,000 generations ago.

00:50:32.120 --> 00:50:34.322
And we show it from
our DNA variation.

00:50:34.322 --> 00:50:35.780
This is, of course,
very important,

00:50:35.780 --> 00:50:37.790
because, if we have a very
little amount of variation,

00:50:37.790 --> 00:50:39.320
we stand a chance
of characterizing

00:50:39.320 --> 00:50:42.080
every variant in the genome,
maybe three or four variants

00:50:42.080 --> 00:50:44.780
for each of 30,000 genes,
and testing each one

00:50:44.780 --> 00:50:46.844
for correlation with disease.

00:50:46.844 --> 00:50:48.260
[? Finding ?] doing
human genetics

00:50:48.260 --> 00:50:50.150
may become a simple
matrix problem.

00:50:50.150 --> 00:50:52.700
Write down all the variants
along the top, all the diseases

00:50:52.700 --> 00:50:55.640
along the side, and fill in
the matrix, and just see.

00:50:55.640 --> 00:50:57.880
That would be a lot of fun.

00:50:57.880 --> 00:50:59.431
Well, if so, in
order to do that,

00:50:59.431 --> 00:51:00.555
we need various techniques.

00:51:00.555 --> 00:51:02.600
I won't fuss over these.

00:51:02.600 --> 00:51:04.700
There are techniques called
"association studies"

00:51:04.700 --> 00:51:06.350
where you directly
test variants.

00:51:06.350 --> 00:51:09.230
Linkage disequilibrium studies,
where you indirectly test them

00:51:09.230 --> 00:51:10.880
by ancestral haplotypes.

00:51:10.880 --> 00:51:12.530
But what you need
for all of that

00:51:12.530 --> 00:51:15.380
is to have zillions and
zillions of variants.

00:51:15.380 --> 00:51:19.070
The goal is to have such a
dense map of single nucleotide

00:51:19.070 --> 00:51:21.300
polymorphisms-- we
call them SNPs--

00:51:21.300 --> 00:51:24.800
that you can comprehensively
test for disease association

00:51:24.800 --> 00:51:26.480
anywhere in the
genome, by looking

00:51:26.480 --> 00:51:28.550
for the signatures of
ancestral haplotypes

00:51:28.550 --> 00:51:30.200
enriched in people with disease.

00:51:30.200 --> 00:51:33.110
But you need a lot of them!

00:51:33.110 --> 00:51:35.840
4,000, that we published
three years ago, is not much.

00:51:35.840 --> 00:51:38.750
So, happily, a great example
of how industry and academia

00:51:38.750 --> 00:51:43.574
can work together, the SNP
Consortium was founded.

00:51:43.574 --> 00:51:45.740
A bunch of companies and a
bunch of academic centers

00:51:45.740 --> 00:51:48.380
got together and said they
would pool their resources

00:51:48.380 --> 00:51:52.156
and find 300,000 variants
across the genome

00:51:52.156 --> 00:51:54.530
and put them all freely in
the public domain without cost

00:51:54.530 --> 00:51:57.320
or any kind of
patent protection.

00:51:57.320 --> 00:51:58.970
This got going
about two years ago,

00:51:58.970 --> 00:52:00.600
and it has been
wildly successful.

00:52:00.600 --> 00:52:03.050
It's a great example of how
industry and academia can

00:52:03.050 --> 00:52:04.820
work together to
produce things that

00:52:04.820 --> 00:52:07.010
are helpful to all concerned.

00:52:07.010 --> 00:52:09.080
In addition, a bunch
of variants come

00:52:09.080 --> 00:52:12.020
from just taking the sequence
and taking our clone-based map

00:52:12.020 --> 00:52:14.070
and looking at the overlaps.

00:52:14.070 --> 00:52:16.400
So the number of
SNPs, the number

00:52:16.400 --> 00:52:18.410
of variants in the
genome, has grown

00:52:18.410 --> 00:52:22.660
from when we reported,
right over here, 4,000,

00:52:22.660 --> 00:52:24.504
and we were very
impressed with ourselves,

00:52:24.504 --> 00:52:26.170
to basically the work
of three centers--

00:52:26.170 --> 00:52:29.280
Wash U, Whitehead, and
the Sanger Centre--

00:52:29.280 --> 00:52:30.110
vvvoomp!

00:52:30.110 --> 00:52:32.480
And appearing
yesterday in Nature

00:52:32.480 --> 00:52:36.680
was the companion paper
reporting 1.42 million variants

00:52:36.680 --> 00:52:38.530
in the human genome.

00:52:38.530 --> 00:52:41.530
We now have a variant, on
average, every 2 kilobases

00:52:41.530 --> 00:52:42.202
in the genome.

00:52:42.202 --> 00:52:43.660
We will push forward,
and we'd like

00:52:43.660 --> 00:52:45.820
to get about 4 billion
variants or so.

00:52:45.820 --> 00:52:47.800
But already we are
just up to our ears

00:52:47.800 --> 00:52:49.360
in variants across the genome.

00:52:49.360 --> 00:52:51.390
And we stand a very
good chance of finding

00:52:51.390 --> 00:52:54.400
all of the ancestral segments
that occurred in the genome

00:52:54.400 --> 00:52:56.280
and using them to trace disease.

00:52:56.280 --> 00:52:57.280
I won't tell you about--

00:52:57.280 --> 00:52:58.960
I'll skip over a
couple slides, here--

00:52:58.960 --> 00:53:01.580
that we've also measured
how big those segments are,

00:53:01.580 --> 00:53:03.079
and that varies
with populations.

00:53:03.079 --> 00:53:04.870
But in northern Europe,
they're quite big--

00:53:04.870 --> 00:53:07.270
on average, about 100
kilobases in total,

00:53:07.270 --> 00:53:09.910
maybe 50 or 60 in either
direction, from a point.

00:53:09.910 --> 00:53:11.380
In certain African
tribes, they're

00:53:11.380 --> 00:53:13.300
much smaller
because the ancestry

00:53:13.300 --> 00:53:15.760
is different in terms
of the age of the family

00:53:15.760 --> 00:53:16.900
of that population.

00:53:16.900 --> 00:53:19.240
But we're going to be able
to understand those and use

00:53:19.240 --> 00:53:20.905
these for disease studies.

00:53:20.905 --> 00:53:22.810
I'll skip over those factoids.

00:53:22.810 --> 00:53:26.620
In addition, and I'm not going
to talk anything about it,

00:53:26.620 --> 00:53:28.600
this work on the genome
is having a huge effect

00:53:28.600 --> 00:53:30.640
on cancer studies, as well.

00:53:30.640 --> 00:53:33.640
It's driving us in cancer
studies, and many other people,

00:53:33.640 --> 00:53:36.100
to look at all the genes
simultaneously in the cell.

00:53:36.100 --> 00:53:38.562
And I think the reporting
now of the full list of genes

00:53:38.562 --> 00:53:40.270
means that we're going
to be able to make

00:53:40.270 --> 00:53:44.320
really good microarrays, to
study the pictures of gene

00:53:44.320 --> 00:53:47.350
expression in cells, finally,
with, for the first time,

00:53:47.350 --> 00:53:49.480
comprehensive arrays.

00:53:49.480 --> 00:53:50.200
All right.

00:53:50.200 --> 00:53:55.070
Well, the goal has
been to try to create

00:53:55.070 --> 00:53:56.660
for biology what the
chemists have had

00:53:56.660 --> 00:53:57.950
for the whole last century--

00:53:57.950 --> 00:53:59.630
a periodic table.

00:53:59.630 --> 00:54:01.040
To have the parts list.

00:54:01.040 --> 00:54:02.640
[INAUDIBLE] folks
call this stuff

00:54:02.640 --> 00:54:04.410
the "holy grail" and stuff.

00:54:04.410 --> 00:54:06.610
I'm not convinced
by any of that.

00:54:06.610 --> 00:54:08.580
This is just a parts list.

00:54:08.580 --> 00:54:12.470
But a parts list is
a very useful thing.

00:54:12.470 --> 00:54:15.050
We are now closer than I
had thought we might ever

00:54:15.050 --> 00:54:17.510
get to such a parts list.

00:54:17.510 --> 00:54:20.450
We don't have fully all the
nucleotides, but we will.

00:54:20.450 --> 00:54:21.710
Give it another year or so.

00:54:21.710 --> 00:54:23.210
We don't have fully
all the genes,

00:54:23.210 --> 00:54:26.150
but we have the vast majority
covered, to, the vast majority,

00:54:26.150 --> 00:54:27.020
their lengths.

00:54:27.020 --> 00:54:29.660
And give it a year or two,
and that will be there.

00:54:29.660 --> 00:54:32.720
And we also have, by the way,
the isotopes, the variants

00:54:32.720 --> 00:54:36.670
on the elements--
namely, those SNPs.

00:54:36.670 --> 00:54:39.610
I think it's probably fair
to say that you could already

00:54:39.610 --> 00:54:42.310
start planning your experiments,
counting on the fact, now,

00:54:42.310 --> 00:54:44.200
safely, that all of
that will be there,

00:54:44.200 --> 00:54:47.080
that the periodic table
will be up on your wall

00:54:47.080 --> 00:54:49.705
in the next year or two.

00:54:49.705 --> 00:54:50.580
Won't be perfect yet.

00:54:50.580 --> 00:54:50.970
It'll take--

00:54:50.970 --> 00:54:52.440
Actually, the periodic
table wasn't perfect,

00:54:52.440 --> 00:54:53.460
when it first went
up on the wall.

00:54:53.460 --> 00:54:55.530
They spent 20 years
filling in all the holes.

00:54:55.530 --> 00:54:56.056
Right?

00:54:56.056 --> 00:54:58.180
It didn't cost them quite
as much money, back then,

00:54:58.180 --> 00:54:59.170
but they spent a
lot of time, filling

00:54:59.170 --> 00:55:00.170
in all the holes, right?

00:55:00.170 --> 00:55:01.140
[LAUGHTER]

00:55:01.140 --> 00:55:03.190
And so none of these
things are ever perfect,

00:55:03.190 --> 00:55:06.360
but the conceptual
framework there is there.

00:55:06.360 --> 00:55:09.660
I think it'll have
a big effect on us.

00:55:09.660 --> 00:55:14.490
Before I close, though,
I have to reemphasize

00:55:14.490 --> 00:55:17.100
what I said at the beginning.

00:55:17.100 --> 00:55:20.100
This was different than any
other project in biology.

00:55:20.100 --> 00:55:22.909
When you stand up usually, and
you give a talk in biology,

00:55:22.909 --> 00:55:24.450
you talk about your
work or your work

00:55:24.450 --> 00:55:25.824
and a couple of
graduate students

00:55:25.824 --> 00:55:27.664
or some postdocs or
something like that.

00:55:27.664 --> 00:55:29.580
This is a project where
there's nobody who can

00:55:29.580 --> 00:55:31.810
take personal credit for this.

00:55:31.810 --> 00:55:34.530
This is something that
happened because of the way

00:55:34.530 --> 00:55:37.410
everyone agreed to pull
together and share the work

00:55:37.410 --> 00:55:39.330
and share the credit.

00:55:39.330 --> 00:55:42.990
The group that did the most
to bring this to fruition

00:55:42.990 --> 00:55:44.400
is shown here, on the snow.

00:55:44.400 --> 00:55:48.820
This is the Whitehead Genome
Center, over in the park,

00:55:48.820 --> 00:55:49.920
in Charles Street.

00:55:49.920 --> 00:55:51.630
This was the only place we
could find we could actually

00:55:51.630 --> 00:55:52.280
take a picture.

00:55:52.280 --> 00:55:53.790
There was no indoor
location we could actually

00:55:53.790 --> 00:55:55.590
manage to get the
whole genome center

00:55:55.590 --> 00:55:58.350
and still have a camera stand
back far enough to get them.

00:55:58.350 --> 00:55:59.860
But this is the folks.

00:55:59.860 --> 00:56:04.670
And one could not ask for
a better set of colleagues

00:56:04.670 --> 00:56:09.560
and friends to have worked with,
over such an extraordinarily

00:56:09.560 --> 00:56:15.140
exciting, at times stressful,
but always exhilarating time.

00:56:15.140 --> 00:56:19.370
And so I am in tremendous
debt to my colleagues, here.

00:56:19.370 --> 00:56:22.910
I will not name the names
of all 250 people associated

00:56:22.910 --> 00:56:23.735
with this.

00:56:23.735 --> 00:56:26.400
I will in particular single
out Lauren Linton, who

00:56:26.400 --> 00:56:30.110
did an amazing job at mounting
a scale-up for the center,

00:56:30.110 --> 00:56:31.599
in the course of this project.

00:56:31.599 --> 00:56:33.890
And I do particularly want
to thank Chad Nusbaum, Bruce

00:56:33.890 --> 00:56:36.650
Birren, and Mike Zody, who have
been the assistant directors

00:56:36.650 --> 00:56:38.930
of the center, who have
managed all different parts

00:56:38.930 --> 00:56:40.680
of this effort.

00:56:40.680 --> 00:56:43.674
But there are heroes,
throughout this entire picture.

00:56:43.674 --> 00:56:45.590
In addition, there are
groups around the world

00:56:45.590 --> 00:56:47.690
that contributed tremendously.

00:56:47.690 --> 00:56:50.720
This is a picture of the first
Bermuda meeting that occurred.

00:56:50.720 --> 00:56:53.630
It was in Bermuda,
really, truly,

00:56:53.630 --> 00:56:55.820
amongst the different
international centers.

00:56:55.820 --> 00:56:57.650
And I owe great
debts to colleagues

00:56:57.650 --> 00:57:00.260
in different countries, and we
all are still working together.

00:57:00.260 --> 00:57:03.530
But the only really appropriate
credit slide for this talk

00:57:03.530 --> 00:57:04.155
is that.

00:57:04.155 --> 00:57:05.780
This is the first
talk where you really

00:57:05.780 --> 00:57:08.150
just have to put up the
whole globe and say,

00:57:08.150 --> 00:57:09.460
we done it together.

00:57:09.460 --> 00:57:11.540
And it was really,
really satisfying,

00:57:11.540 --> 00:57:14.450
to go to Washington this week,
with so many of our friends,

00:57:14.450 --> 00:57:17.810
and realize that, for
something that we had started--

00:57:17.810 --> 00:57:18.950
in my case, many cases--

00:57:18.950 --> 00:57:22.020
15 years ago, we
actually had done it--

00:57:22.020 --> 00:57:24.364
not all quite finished,
but we'd done it

00:57:24.364 --> 00:57:26.780
well enough that we knew there
was now no doubt it was all

00:57:26.780 --> 00:57:28.070
going to get done.

00:57:28.070 --> 00:57:31.440
And I confess I tend to be
pretty inured to these sorts

00:57:31.440 --> 00:57:31.940
of things.

00:57:31.940 --> 00:57:34.445
I'm not, you know, one to
get soppy about this stuff.

00:57:34.445 --> 00:57:35.820
But, by the end
of day on Monday,

00:57:35.820 --> 00:57:39.970
I had realized that this is
kind of the surprise, in a way,

00:57:39.970 --> 00:57:45.211
is that only in retrospect do
you realize how history ends up

00:57:45.211 --> 00:57:45.710
happening.

00:57:45.710 --> 00:57:48.200
It happens sort of just by
doing your work every day,

00:57:48.200 --> 00:57:49.850
on something worth working on.

00:57:49.850 --> 00:57:53.090
And, 15 years later, we all
who have been working together,

00:57:53.090 --> 00:57:54.850
here in this community,
on something,

00:57:54.850 --> 00:57:57.350
just working on the right next
step and the right next step,

00:57:57.350 --> 00:58:00.800
realize that, over 15 years,
collectively, we all did

00:58:00.800 --> 00:58:02.610
manage to make
quite a difference.

00:58:02.610 --> 00:58:05.170
And so it's really fun to
come, this week in particular,

00:58:05.170 --> 00:58:06.890
and just share it
with the community.

00:58:06.890 --> 00:58:08.030
Because it's all of ours.

00:58:08.030 --> 00:58:09.446
Thanks very much.

00:58:09.446 --> 00:58:14.276
[APPLAUSE]

00:58:14.276 --> 00:58:20.072
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:58:20.072 --> 00:58:22.290
SHARP: Do you have many
of your colleagues here?

00:58:22.290 --> 00:58:24.373
LANDER: There are some,
at least, right over here.

00:58:24.373 --> 00:58:25.602
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:58:25.602 --> 00:58:28.060
LANDER: Yeah, I don't know how
many [? of them, but ?] yes.

00:58:28.060 --> 00:58:36.390
[APPLAUSE]

00:58:36.390 --> 00:58:39.310
SHARP: In the tone
of Eric's comments,

00:58:39.310 --> 00:58:41.740
I'd like to everybody
who participated

00:58:41.740 --> 00:58:44.470
in the genome-center work
who's in the room to stand up.

00:58:44.470 --> 00:58:45.880
And let's thank them, too.

00:58:45.880 --> 00:58:47.178
Come on!

00:58:47.178 --> 00:58:50.636
[APPLAUSE]

00:58:50.636 --> 00:58:51.624
LANDER: [INAUDIBLE]!

00:59:02.992 --> 00:59:05.200
SHARP: Eric would be pleased
to take a few questions.

00:59:05.200 --> 00:59:07.720
This is a big crowd, but we
can take a few questions.

00:59:07.720 --> 00:59:09.525
LANDER: Not from
people who I work with.

00:59:09.525 --> 00:59:09.820
[LAUGHTER]

00:59:09.820 --> 00:59:10.930
SHARP: No, not from people who--

00:59:10.930 --> 00:59:12.263
LANDER: You can ask me tomorrow!

00:59:12.263 --> 00:59:13.720
[LAUGHTER]

00:59:13.720 --> 00:59:15.812
SHARP: A few-- please.

00:59:15.812 --> 00:59:18.145
AUDIENCE: [? How many ?]
[? of the genes are ?] involved

00:59:18.145 --> 00:59:19.960
in intelligence?

00:59:19.960 --> 00:59:22.480
LANDER: The fraction of genes
involved in intelligence.

00:59:22.480 --> 00:59:24.505
[LAUGHTER]

00:59:24.505 --> 00:59:25.825
It depends on the definition.

00:59:25.825 --> 00:59:27.531
SHARP: [INAUDIBLE]

00:59:27.531 --> 00:59:29.530
LANDER: If you mean the
fraction of genes which,

00:59:29.530 --> 00:59:32.260
when knocked out, disrupt
your intelligence,

00:59:32.260 --> 00:59:35.710
it's probably like we've
always taught-- half, or more.

00:59:35.710 --> 00:59:37.262
If you include
being dead as being

00:59:37.262 --> 00:59:38.470
an insult your intelligence--

00:59:38.470 --> 00:59:39.050
[LAUGHTER]

00:59:39.050 --> 00:59:40.780
--then it's a very
large fraction.

00:59:40.780 --> 00:59:43.000
If you want to
know what fraction

00:59:43.000 --> 00:59:46.510
of the variation in
intelligence in the population

00:59:46.510 --> 00:59:50.200
is attributable to variation
in genes, we haven't a clue.

00:59:50.200 --> 00:59:51.700
And my own guess is--

00:59:51.700 --> 00:59:53.440
I mean, this is the
usual question of,

00:59:53.440 --> 00:59:56.060
how much genetic determinism
of intelligence there is--

00:59:56.060 --> 00:59:58.990
there's nothing in our sequence
that tells us much about that.

00:59:58.990 --> 01:00:02.290
And what I do think I find most
interesting in our sequence,

01:00:02.290 --> 01:00:04.870
because I think it's
a point worth making,

01:00:04.870 --> 01:00:08.440
is that the very fact that we
are so extraordinarily similar,

01:00:08.440 --> 01:00:11.634
across the species, that
we're 99.9% identical,

01:00:11.634 --> 01:00:14.050
only one difference in 1,000--
this holds across the whole

01:00:14.050 --> 01:00:14.750
planet--

01:00:14.750 --> 01:00:17.890
and that we come from such
a small founding group,

01:00:17.890 --> 01:00:24.570
tells me that the
population genetics have not

01:00:24.570 --> 01:00:28.950
changed in 5,000
generations but we

01:00:28.950 --> 01:00:32.239
ourselves have changed
opportunity [? and ?] society

01:00:32.239 --> 01:00:33.780
and what's possible
and what we think

01:00:33.780 --> 01:00:36.660
men or women or people from
different groups can do,

01:00:36.660 --> 01:00:38.350
so dramatically.

01:00:38.350 --> 01:00:42.360
So I must say, realizing how
the gene pool has not undergone

01:00:42.360 --> 01:00:44.130
significant reshaping,
in that huge time,

01:00:44.130 --> 01:00:47.460
and yet society can come out
so different-- that is to say,

01:00:47.460 --> 01:00:49.590
what we do with our
brains and what we do with

01:00:49.590 --> 01:00:52.640
our intelligence has so many
degrees of freedom, there,

01:00:52.640 --> 01:00:55.140
that I'm not too worried about
what the genome is ever going

01:00:55.140 --> 01:00:57.990
to tell us about limits on
what we can possibly do,

01:00:57.990 --> 01:00:59.856
because we have
tremendous evidence that,

01:00:59.856 --> 01:01:01.230
within whatever
limits there are,

01:01:01.230 --> 01:01:02.899
we've been able to
have huge variations,

01:01:02.899 --> 01:01:04.690
[? and ?] [? a lot's ?]
within our control.

01:01:07.980 --> 01:01:08.990
SHARP: Over here?

01:01:08.990 --> 01:01:09.870
Oh, here, first.

01:01:09.870 --> 01:01:11.818
LANDER: Sorry, uh, yeah.

01:01:11.818 --> 01:01:16.688
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
30,000, 40,000 genes.

01:01:16.688 --> 01:01:19.610
What about all those companies
that are [INAUDIBLE] marketing,

01:01:19.610 --> 01:01:23.985
you know, we have a
database [INAUDIBLE]..

01:01:23.985 --> 01:01:25.110
SHARP: Repeat the question.

01:01:25.110 --> 01:01:27.680
LANDER: So, given that there
appear to be only 30,000,

01:01:27.680 --> 01:01:30.530
40,000 genes, the question is
that there are, for example,

01:01:30.530 --> 01:01:33.650
ads in several journals
that say, subscribe

01:01:33.650 --> 01:01:37.125
to the X database,
which has 120,000 genes.

01:01:37.125 --> 01:01:39.350
[LAUGHTER]

01:01:39.350 --> 01:01:42.260
So, let's see-- the
markets have closed.

01:01:42.260 --> 01:01:45.230
I could say, well, one response
potentially would be to sell.

01:01:45.230 --> 01:01:46.023
Right?

01:01:46.023 --> 01:01:52.440
[LAUGHTER]

01:01:52.440 --> 01:01:55.320
Another response would be that
the New York Times actually

01:01:55.320 --> 01:01:57.942
has begun to probe Insight
Pharmaceuticals, now called

01:01:57.942 --> 01:01:59.400
Insight Genomics,
on this question.

01:01:59.400 --> 01:02:01.470
Because the ad I just
quoted is from Insight.

01:02:01.470 --> 01:02:03.250
And, according to
the New York Times,

01:02:03.250 --> 01:02:04.411
the chairman of Insight--

01:02:04.411 --> 01:02:06.660
I think it was the chairman--
the president of Insight

01:02:06.660 --> 01:02:09.201
said to the New York Times this
week that, oh-oh, we actually

01:02:09.201 --> 01:02:12.510
meant 120,000
distinct transcripts,

01:02:12.510 --> 01:02:15.820
but they actually come
from 40,000 genes.

01:02:15.820 --> 01:02:18.360
So, in fact, that one,
if I take correctly

01:02:18.360 --> 01:02:20.040
what was in the New
York Times, appears

01:02:20.040 --> 01:02:21.600
to have just been retracted.

01:02:21.600 --> 01:02:24.120
And it was just the detail,
that they meant transcripts

01:02:24.120 --> 01:02:25.050
not genes.

01:02:25.050 --> 01:02:27.300
But Bill Haseltine at
Human Genome Sciences

01:02:27.300 --> 01:02:30.930
is rock-solid sure that
he's got 120,000 genes

01:02:30.930 --> 01:02:33.930
and that he's not giving
any ground on that.

01:02:33.930 --> 01:02:37.590
And so I propose the following
really fun challenge.

01:02:37.590 --> 01:02:40.530
We get to specify
1% of the genome,

01:02:40.530 --> 01:02:41.940
and anybody who
wants to disagree

01:02:41.940 --> 01:02:43.530
produces the genes
they claim they

01:02:43.530 --> 01:02:46.326
have in the 1% that we picked.

01:02:46.326 --> 01:02:48.450
We'll check them together
and see if they're right.

01:02:48.450 --> 01:02:51.060
If they have three times
as many genes in that 1%,

01:02:51.060 --> 01:02:53.774
then we'll believe that they've
got gold in the other 99%.

01:02:53.774 --> 01:02:55.440
But if nobody wants
to take us up on it,

01:02:55.440 --> 01:02:58.780
it's tough to deal
with the claims.

01:02:58.780 --> 01:03:00.468
Yes.

01:03:00.468 --> 01:03:04.507
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:03:04.507 --> 01:03:06.340
LANDER: Future priorities
for other species.

01:03:06.340 --> 01:03:09.000
Well, what this is telling
us is that sequencing

01:03:09.000 --> 01:03:11.340
is a tremendously powerful
way to get information

01:03:11.340 --> 01:03:12.470
you never imagined before.

01:03:12.470 --> 01:03:14.210
Like, all these
repeats, all this stuff.

01:03:14.210 --> 01:03:16.170
Clearly, experiment is
never going go go away.

01:03:16.170 --> 01:03:18.100
Experiment is our
primary driving force.

01:03:18.100 --> 01:03:20.280
But it's a great way
to form hypotheses.

01:03:20.280 --> 01:03:23.400
So my sense is, already mouse
is underway, rat is underway,

01:03:23.400 --> 01:03:25.410
zebrafish is underway,
two different pufferfish

01:03:25.410 --> 01:03:27.270
are underway.

01:03:27.270 --> 01:03:29.460
I believe that several
of the organisms

01:03:29.460 --> 01:03:32.450
along the lineage leading to
vertebrates, like [? Ciona ?]

01:03:32.450 --> 01:03:36.960
and such, will be sequenced
in the next couple of years,

01:03:36.960 --> 01:03:39.180
in order to figure out what
were the key innovations.

01:03:39.180 --> 01:03:41.839
Chimp is a very
interesting debate.

01:03:41.839 --> 01:03:43.380
I can tell you
already, there's going

01:03:43.380 --> 01:03:45.712
to be 50 million
differences with chimp,

01:03:45.712 --> 01:03:47.670
because we know what the
percent difference is.

01:03:47.670 --> 01:03:49.620
I just don't know which ones
are going to be important.

01:03:49.620 --> 01:03:50.970
And there's an active
debate in the community

01:03:50.970 --> 01:03:53.220
as to whether we should
race to sequence chimp

01:03:53.220 --> 01:03:54.480
or that we should figure
out what in the world

01:03:54.480 --> 01:03:56.220
we'd do if we had the
50 million differences.

01:03:56.220 --> 01:03:57.370
And I think what'll happen
is there'll probably

01:03:57.370 --> 01:04:00.180
be light coverage of the chimp,
to see if we can interpret

01:04:00.180 --> 01:04:01.110
those differences.

01:04:01.110 --> 01:04:02.740
Most of them are
probably just noise.

01:04:02.740 --> 01:04:03.240
Right?

01:04:03.240 --> 01:04:05.342
And, if we can figure out,
is it 10 that mattered,

01:04:05.342 --> 01:04:07.050
or 10,000 that mattered,
how are we going

01:04:07.050 --> 01:04:08.740
to know before we go overboard?

01:04:08.740 --> 01:04:11.700
I have a feeling that, as the
cost of sequencing comes down--

01:04:11.700 --> 01:04:13.290
already, yeasts!

01:04:13.290 --> 01:04:14.910
And it's no big
deal to do bacteria.

01:04:14.910 --> 01:04:15.240
Right?

01:04:15.240 --> 01:04:17.550
And I think it's going soon
be no big deal to do fungi.

01:04:17.550 --> 01:04:20.670
And I can't see why
we wouldn't just

01:04:20.670 --> 01:04:22.350
continue to sequence
lots of organisms,

01:04:22.350 --> 01:04:24.307
as the cost comes
down, because it's

01:04:24.307 --> 01:04:26.640
telling us what is the greatest
lab notebook in history.

01:04:26.640 --> 01:04:28.050
Evolution, it
turns out, has been

01:04:28.050 --> 01:04:30.600
taking notes for 3
and 1/2 billion years,

01:04:30.600 --> 01:04:31.740
in its lab notebook.

01:04:31.740 --> 01:04:34.600
Now, it did not have a good
course on research practices,

01:04:34.600 --> 01:04:35.100
because--

01:04:35.100 --> 01:04:35.820
[LAUGHTER]

01:04:35.820 --> 01:04:38.382
--because it only takes notes
on the successful experiments.

01:04:38.382 --> 01:04:40.215
It throws away the
unsuccessful experiments.

01:04:40.215 --> 01:04:41.160
[LAUGHTER]

01:04:41.160 --> 01:04:43.330
But we got the notes on
the successful experiments.

01:04:43.330 --> 01:04:45.371
I mean, obviously, we
don't want to criticize it,

01:04:45.371 --> 01:04:47.871
because it got started before
these procedures were required

01:04:47.871 --> 01:04:49.204
of graduate students, et cetera.

01:04:49.204 --> 01:04:50.040
[LAUGHTER]

01:04:50.040 --> 01:04:51.876
But it's a pretty
good lab notebook.

01:04:51.876 --> 01:04:53.000
SHARP: Over here, there's--

01:04:53.000 --> 01:04:53.690
LANDER: Yeah.

01:04:53.690 --> 01:04:54.380
SHARP: --and
[? then-- ?] question.

01:04:54.380 --> 01:04:56.400
We will end in a
couple more questions.

01:04:56.400 --> 01:04:58.442
AUDIENCE: Yeah, I may
have missed a quote, when

01:04:58.442 --> 01:05:00.648
you explained how you
were going to apply,

01:05:00.648 --> 01:05:02.135
once you have the
sequenced genome,

01:05:02.135 --> 01:05:05.032
how do you use that to--

01:05:05.032 --> 01:05:06.497
how would you apply
that knowledge?

01:05:06.497 --> 01:05:08.205
Like, once you have
the sequenced genome,

01:05:08.205 --> 01:05:09.115
how do you use it?

01:05:09.115 --> 01:05:10.030
I'm not sure I--

01:05:10.030 --> 01:05:10.770
LANDER: okay.

01:05:10.770 --> 01:05:15.520
So what I didn't really
say, and I apologize,

01:05:15.520 --> 01:05:16.880
in many different ways.

01:05:16.880 --> 01:05:21.870
One way, make an expression
array, where you put down

01:05:21.870 --> 01:05:24.810
a piece of DNA
complementary to every gene,

01:05:24.810 --> 01:05:27.720
take the messengers from a
cell, wash them over, and see

01:05:27.720 --> 01:05:28.890
which genes are on and off.

01:05:28.890 --> 01:05:31.110
By having a comprehensive
list of all genes,

01:05:31.110 --> 01:05:33.310
you know you have a description.

01:05:33.310 --> 01:05:35.080
Here's another way.

01:05:35.080 --> 01:05:37.660
Produce full-length
CDNAs for every gene,

01:05:37.660 --> 01:05:40.210
and actually express the
proteins in appropriate places.

01:05:40.210 --> 01:05:41.830
If you're a
pharmaceutical company,

01:05:41.830 --> 01:05:44.620
test a small molecule that
has an interesting biological

01:05:44.620 --> 01:05:48.310
action, but you don't know
what its target is, for binding

01:05:48.310 --> 01:05:50.590
against all 30,000 genes.

01:05:50.590 --> 01:05:52.240
And, if it has a
side effect, test it

01:05:52.240 --> 01:05:55.240
against all 30,000 genes to
find out what other things it

01:05:55.240 --> 01:05:56.800
may bind to.

01:05:56.800 --> 01:05:58.920
Look at all the interactions
between those things.

01:05:58.920 --> 01:06:01.240
I mean, in some
sense, it's asking,

01:06:01.240 --> 01:06:02.620
why have a periodic table?

01:06:02.620 --> 01:06:04.557
After all, it doesn't
define the experiment.

01:06:04.557 --> 01:06:06.640
The genome sequence doesn't
define the experiment.

01:06:06.640 --> 01:06:08.260
What it does, though,
is it converts

01:06:08.260 --> 01:06:13.300
the search for every answer
into a search in a finite list.

01:06:13.300 --> 01:06:14.860
It means, with the
periodic table,

01:06:14.860 --> 01:06:16.090
I can look it up
on the mass spec.

01:06:16.090 --> 01:06:18.673
If I know what I saw go by, and
its mass and a few properties,

01:06:18.673 --> 01:06:19.810
I know what it was.

01:06:19.810 --> 01:06:22.060
Increasingly, in biology,
we're going to be in a world

01:06:22.060 --> 01:06:24.400
where, when we see
something happen,

01:06:24.400 --> 01:06:26.590
we can know what it
was that just happened,

01:06:26.590 --> 01:06:29.020
what particle went by,
what protein it just was.

01:06:29.020 --> 01:06:32.110
Whereas, in the past, almost
any phenomenon we were studying,

01:06:32.110 --> 01:06:35.290
we had to spend months
or years figuring out

01:06:35.290 --> 01:06:36.980
what just did that.

01:06:36.980 --> 01:06:40.206
And I think what it
is is the great boon

01:06:40.206 --> 01:06:41.830
to the graduate
student of the postdoc,

01:06:41.830 --> 01:06:44.163
because usually the graduate
students and postdocs spend

01:06:44.163 --> 01:06:46.414
most of their time tracking
down what the part was.

01:06:46.414 --> 01:06:48.580
Which is kind of boring,
compared to the phenomenon.

01:06:48.580 --> 01:06:50.110
Now they can study a phenomenon.

01:06:50.110 --> 01:06:52.359
And the tools aren't all
there, but four or five years

01:06:52.359 --> 01:06:54.400
from now, maybe,
it'll be much easier

01:06:54.400 --> 01:06:56.620
to describe that phenomenon
[? in ?] working parts

01:06:56.620 --> 01:06:58.100
and get on to real experiments.

01:06:58.100 --> 01:07:00.520
That's what it's for.

01:07:00.520 --> 01:07:02.940
SHARP: [INAUDIBLE]

01:07:02.940 --> 01:07:07.480
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] what you
think it's going to happen now,

01:07:07.480 --> 01:07:08.200
how many--

01:07:08.200 --> 01:07:10.001
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:07:10.001 --> 01:07:11.880
LANDER: We sort of--

01:07:11.880 --> 01:07:13.410
one gene, one protein.

01:07:13.410 --> 01:07:14.784
What happens to that?

01:07:14.784 --> 01:07:16.450
Well, of course, "one
gene, one protein"

01:07:16.450 --> 01:07:19.210
is the official doctrine in 701.

01:07:19.210 --> 01:07:21.820
But, even in 701,
we did acknowledge

01:07:21.820 --> 01:07:23.980
that that wasn't always
the case and that there

01:07:23.980 --> 01:07:25.750
was alternative splicing.

01:07:25.750 --> 01:07:28.220
I mean, viruses have been
good at this for a long time,

01:07:28.220 --> 01:07:31.549
of being able to put together
other transcripts and such.

01:07:31.549 --> 01:07:33.340
And we've known about
this for a long time.

01:07:33.340 --> 01:07:36.340
What it means is that there
will be a more intensive study

01:07:36.340 --> 01:07:38.170
to figure out, when
you make three proteins

01:07:38.170 --> 01:07:41.042
from the same gene, how
different are they, really?

01:07:41.042 --> 01:07:42.750
They're not radically
different proteins.

01:07:42.750 --> 01:07:44.620
They're variations on
a theme, typically,

01:07:44.620 --> 01:07:45.940
with some extra domain.

01:07:45.940 --> 01:07:48.730
And I think some [? serious ?]
work will have to go to that

01:07:48.730 --> 01:07:51.310
question, of what this
domain's-- you know,

01:07:51.310 --> 01:07:53.200
the extra domains
do accomplish there.

01:07:53.200 --> 01:07:56.890
But I think, as
with all of biology,

01:07:56.890 --> 01:07:58.907
I mean, the central
dogma isn't strictly

01:07:58.907 --> 01:08:01.240
right, and "one gene, one
protein" isn't strictly right,

01:08:01.240 --> 01:08:03.260
but, you know, it's not bad.

01:08:03.260 --> 01:08:04.463
It's not bad.

01:08:04.463 --> 01:08:06.424
SHARP: okay, this is
the last question.

01:08:06.424 --> 01:08:07.340
LANDER: Last question.

01:08:07.340 --> 01:08:08.215
SHARP: Last question.

01:08:08.215 --> 01:08:10.048
AUDIENCE: How well were
you able to sequence

01:08:10.048 --> 01:08:12.410
over the centromeric
and telomeric regions?

01:08:12.410 --> 01:08:14.770
Were you able to
[? define ?] [INAUDIBLE]??

01:08:14.770 --> 01:08:17.149
[LAUGHTER]

01:08:17.149 --> 01:08:19.040
LANDER: So the BACs
that were sequenced

01:08:19.040 --> 01:08:24.359
were prioritized not to be those
in highly repeated regions.

01:08:24.359 --> 01:08:26.479
We do seem to recover a
decent number of clones

01:08:26.479 --> 01:08:27.630
for those regions.

01:08:27.630 --> 01:08:30.229
But, because they tend
to be strongly repeated,

01:08:30.229 --> 01:08:31.859
they got put at the
bottom of the list.

01:08:31.859 --> 01:08:33.800
And so we poke
our way, our head,

01:08:33.800 --> 01:08:36.012
into the telomeric and
the centromeric regions,

01:08:36.012 --> 01:08:37.220
but we haven't dealt with it.

01:08:37.220 --> 01:08:38.960
And I'm not sure how well we're
ever going to do it, really,

01:08:38.960 --> 01:08:41.490
cleaning up two megabases
of centromeric repeat,

01:08:41.490 --> 01:08:42.743
or whether it matters.

01:08:42.743 --> 01:08:44.675
SHARP: Last question
[INAUDIBLE]..

01:08:44.675 --> 01:08:46.124
AUDIENCE: So, many
of the genomes

01:08:46.124 --> 01:08:51.160
that [? can ?] sequenced,
above 50% of the genes

01:08:51.160 --> 01:08:52.779
have no known function.

01:08:52.779 --> 01:08:55.120
Is this the same in
the human genome?

01:08:55.120 --> 01:08:56.950
And what steps can
be taken to identify

01:08:56.950 --> 01:08:59.279
the functions of those genes.

01:08:59.279 --> 01:09:01.569
LANDER: So that's a
tremendously important question.

01:09:01.569 --> 01:09:05.109
In most genomes that have been
sequenced, 50% of the genes

01:09:05.109 --> 01:09:06.729
have no known function.

01:09:06.729 --> 01:09:08.020
Is that true for the human?

01:09:08.020 --> 01:09:10.550
And what steps are we taking?

01:09:10.550 --> 01:09:15.550
Ah-- depends what you mean
by "no known function."

01:09:15.550 --> 01:09:17.890
If you say, what
fraction of the genes

01:09:17.890 --> 01:09:20.480
have no recognizable
domain that we could--

01:09:20.480 --> 01:09:23.590
you know, being a protein
kinase, I suppose,

01:09:23.590 --> 01:09:24.670
is a function.

01:09:24.670 --> 01:09:27.850
But, then again, for what
purpose is it a protein kinase?

01:09:27.850 --> 01:09:30.880
So, if you're willing to say,
I know it's a protein kinase,

01:09:30.880 --> 01:09:34.120
or it does-- you
know, it's a GPCR--

01:09:34.120 --> 01:09:35.170
that counts enough--

01:09:35.170 --> 01:09:37.600
60% of the genes we can
attach functions to.

01:09:37.600 --> 01:09:40.210
Some bit of biochemistry
associated with the gene.

01:09:40.210 --> 01:09:42.850
If you're asking, what's
their real role in the body,

01:09:42.850 --> 01:09:48.779
in some meaningful sense, oh,
golly, it might be you know,

01:09:48.779 --> 01:09:50.917
12,000 or so, might be
a third of the genes,

01:09:50.917 --> 01:09:52.500
that we have pretty
good ideas of what

01:09:52.500 --> 01:09:53.640
they're doing in the body.

01:09:53.640 --> 01:09:56.250
And, of course, even there
we're often surprised.

01:09:56.250 --> 01:09:56.900
Right?

01:09:56.900 --> 01:09:59.610
You know, apolipoprotein E
that was carrying around,

01:09:59.610 --> 01:10:03.030
you know, lipoproteins
in our blood system

01:10:03.030 --> 01:10:06.170
turned out to be in our
brain and predispose

01:10:06.170 --> 01:10:07.479
to Alzheimer's disease.

01:10:07.479 --> 01:10:08.520
So it's pretty primitive.

01:10:08.520 --> 01:10:10.561
We don't know what most
of these genes really do.

01:10:10.561 --> 01:10:13.800
That is the work, I
think, of the next decade,

01:10:13.800 --> 01:10:15.450
is to attempt to
find as many ways

01:10:15.450 --> 01:10:17.820
to characterize these
genes by where they're

01:10:17.820 --> 01:10:20.140
localized in the body,
under what circumstances

01:10:20.140 --> 01:10:22.140
their transcripts are
turned on, you know,

01:10:22.140 --> 01:10:25.080
where they are subcellularly
what they interact

01:10:25.080 --> 01:10:27.210
with through partners,
what happens when I make

01:10:27.210 --> 01:10:29.587
a small molecule that
binds to each of them,

01:10:29.587 --> 01:10:31.170
to figure out what
function I disrupt.

01:10:31.170 --> 01:10:34.710
By no means is this a
comprehensive description

01:10:34.710 --> 01:10:35.820
of life.

01:10:35.820 --> 01:10:37.020
This is really--

01:10:37.020 --> 01:10:39.080
I mean, I'm fond of calling it--

01:10:39.080 --> 01:10:42.420
it's the parts list
to a Boeing 777.

01:10:42.420 --> 01:10:45.522
You know all the screws, you
know all the seat cushions,

01:10:45.522 --> 01:10:47.730
you've got the fuel gauge,
you've got all the pieces,

01:10:47.730 --> 01:10:49.751
and they're all spread
out on the floor.

01:10:49.751 --> 01:10:50.250
Right?

01:10:50.250 --> 01:10:52.041
We don't yet know how
to screw it together,

01:10:52.041 --> 01:10:54.030
and we don't know
why it flies, but--

01:10:54.030 --> 01:10:55.680
[LAUGHTER]

01:10:55.680 --> 01:10:58.800
--it's sure better than if we
didn't have the parts list.

01:10:58.800 --> 01:11:00.762
I shall close, with that.

01:11:00.762 --> 01:11:01.470
SHARP: Thank you.

01:11:01.470 --> 01:11:03.320
[APPLAUSE]

